ing, while NVE threshold sensitivity achieves AUROC of at least 0.83. The uncertainty-gated truncation approach shows promising pilot results. Hopper Hop confirms CIVO across a third DMC environment.\n\nNow I'm ready to structure this into the full YAML output with concise narratives and expected results for each experiment. Now I'm working through the specific experiment configurations, pulling in the actual data I have from previous runs. For plan_011 on Walker Walk, I've got concrete results from seeds 1 and 2 showing different failure modes—one collapsed while the other self-corrected—so I'm using those to calibrate my expected ranges for NVE peaks and collapse predictions.\n\nFor plan_012, seed 1 got killed by infrastructure issues so I'm projecting what a re-run would look like without the dt_embedding, expecting the critic to struggle more without that conditioning signal. Seed 2 actually stayed below the 1.5x threshold and recovered, which tells me something about how the architecture handles reward estimation without that component.\n\nPlan_014 already has data—seed 1 performed well at 869.9 with low NVE, but seed 2 collapsed hard into what looks like a different failure mode entirely, one that's mechanistically distinct from the CIVO pattern I've been tracking. Plan_013 hasn't run yet, but I'm predicting both seeds will show value recovery similar to the baseline, staying well below 1.0x NVE since the untrained dt_embedding should prevent that problematic feedback loop from forming.\n\nFor plan_015, the frozen dt_embedding experiment confirms my hypothesis—NVE stayed suppressed around 0.4, the actor hit gradient starvation, and eval was all over the place. The key insight is that a static random embedding means the critic gets constant noise per dt value and can never learn systematic overestimation, so CIVO doesn't emerge, but the actor also starves because it's receiving garbage signals. Plan_016 is next with seeds 3-4, and I'm expecting similar patterns