nThe value prediction distributions reveal that collapsed configs systematically overestimate returns with right-skewed distributions, while healthy ones align predictions with actual returns, and the trained dt_embedding at dt=2 introduces a consistent positive bias in value targets because it's learning to encode multi-step conditioning that the RSSM fundamentally can't support.\n\nFor the dt_embedding similarity analysis, the embeddings start nearly identical at initialization but diverge during training, with this divergence accelerating before collapse in the failed runs while remaining stable in the healthy untrained configs—the training process is forcing the embeddings to differentiate the dt values, which appears to be driving the instability.\n\nNow looking at the NVE threshold sensitivity, the 1.5x multiplier is the sweet spot with AUROC above 0.95 for Walker Walk and cross-environment performance around 0.83, since it sits roughly 2 standard deviations above the baseline noise floor and anything lower gets swamped by natural value-error fluctuations.\n\nThe Cartpole Swingup baseline shows similar performance characteristics to Walker Walk with mean evaluation around 820±25 and comparable value-error calibration across the three seeds.\n\nWhen I add the dt embedding to Cartpole Swingup, all three seeds trigger the NVE threshold by 35K steps with peaks reaching 1.8-3.5x, and some seeds collapse entirely—suggesting that CIVO is a fundamental architectural issue rather than task-dependent, and the simpler observation space in Cartpole might expose this vulnerability faster than the locomotion task.\n\nRemoving the dt embedding dimension entirely eliminates the NVE spikes across all seeds, keeping them well below the 1.5x threshold in the 0.4-0.9x range.\n\nThe eval performance at 500-700 steps by 105K follows the same causal control pattern I'm seeing across Walker Walk and Cheetah Run.\n\nWhen I gate the imagination truncation by uncertainty, it shows promise—truncating at high uncertainty prevents the worst overestimation and keeps NVE below 1.5x—but the tradeoff is that eval performance doesn't quite match the dt=1 baseline since truncation reduces the effective planning horizon.\n\nHopper Hop serves as a third DMC environment to validate that CIVO generalizes: trained dt embedding produces NVE spikes above 1.5x while removing it drops performance below 1.0x, confirming this isn't specific to any single reward structure or dynamics.\n\nNow I'm checking which environments actually confirm CIVO under the best tier—I need three total. Walker Walk and Cheetah Run are locked in, and the third comes from either PickCube in the registry experiment or Cartpole Swingup from the earlier plans. The core hypotheses I'm validating are that Cheetah Run replicates the NVE spike with trained dt embedding at 35K+, that PickCube shows CIVO despite its different observation and action spaces, that full-SGS keeps NVE at or below 1.0 across both environments, and that the 1.5x threshold reliably separates the two conditions in at least three out of four cases.\n\nWriting the YAML predictions now...\n\nI need to verify which experiments already have registry data versus those that are truly planned with no results yet—plan_011 and plan_012 have partial completion, plan_014 is fully done, and I should map out the rest before finalizing the metrics. I'm noting the task differences across environments—Cheetah Run has lower-dimensional observations and actions compared to Walker Walk, while Cartpole Swingup is much simpler with just 5-dim observations and 1-dim action. Now I need to structure the full prediction output as a single YAML block, making sure to capture the cross-environment dynamics and the Full-SGS tier predictions accurately. the user specifically asked for predictions only on planned experiments listed in the experiment_plan, so I'll focus on those and note any gaps in the narrative guidance. Now I'm structuring this as a comprehensive YAML block and need to estimate the Cheetah Run metrics more carefully—the baseline typically converges around 800-900, with a v/e ratio similar to Walker Walk at maybe 0.65-0.75 by convergence, though the early-stage ratio at 35K steps might be slightly different.\n\nFor the CIVO variant on Cheetah Run, I'm expecting the trained dt_emb to produce similar dynamics to what we saw in Walker Walk—a peak eval somewhere in the 300-500 range before potential collapse, with NVE ratios at 35K+ hitting 1.6-3.5x depending on the tier, and collapse occurring in roughly 1-2 out of 3 seeds based on the cross_env_civo_collapse_rate. Looking at the Walker Walk CIVO results as a reference point, the peak performance ratio between CIVO and baseline ranged from about 0.17 at H=8 to 0.51 at H=15, so I'll use that to calibrate my Cheetah Run predictions.\n\nFor H=8 on Cheetah Run, I'm estimating the CIVO peak around 200-350 with post-collapse performance dropping to 50-150. Without the dt_emb, I'd expect recovery similar to Walker Walk's 008f run—roughly 55% of baseline by 105K, putting Cheetah around 400-500. With untrained dt_emb, the pattern from Walker Walk's 008a suggests we'd see 80-90% of baseline recovery, so maybe 650-750 for Cheetah. I'm applying the same rough estimates to Cartpole Swingup, expecting CIVO peaks of 200-400 before collapse, then 400-550 without dt_emb. For plan_034 on NVE threshold sensitivity, I'm computing this from the existing data.\n\nNow I'm looking at the GPU costs and expected results across the remaining plans. Plan_031 on actor entropy is free since it's just analyzing existing logs, plan_032 on value prediction distribution needs about an hour of GPU time to load and evaluate checkpoints, and plan_033 on cosine similarity is also free. I need to structure the expected results properly in YAML format with specific metric values for each plan.\n\nLet me verify the full list of experiment IDs across all groups and make sure I have the right 