{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DpbUYT97kPJ5HSTeknx2GK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing: Diagnostic Analysis of Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL\n2\t\n3\t## Title Candidates\n4\t\n5\t1. **\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination\"**\n6\t2. ~~**\"The Delusional Critic Problem in DreamerV3's Variable-Rate Imagination\"**~~ (deprecated — \"delusional critic\" conflicts with Lu et al. 2018)\n7\t3. **\"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"** ← preferred after D037 terminology decision\n8\t4. **\"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"**\n9\t\n10\t**⚠️ D037 术语决定**: \"delusional critic\" → **\"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"** (简称 CIVO)。Lu et al. 2018 的 delusional bias 指 policy-class constraints 导致不可实现的 Q 值，本文机制是 model imprecision + learned conditioning amplification，概念不同。Dream 模拟三档审稿一致指出术语误导。全文替换：\"delusional critic\" → \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"，可 cite Lu et al. 2018 as analogy but must explicitly differentiate。\n11\t\n12\t**⚠️ D037 TAWM 对比（关键 novelty 升级）**: TAWM (Nhu et al., ICML 2025) 在 TD-MPC2 (MPC setting) 中成功使用 dt-conditioning。本文发现 dt-conditioning 在 imagination-based actor-critic 中失败 → **novelty 从 \"known failure class 的 specific instance\" 升级为 \"dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination — here's the architecture-specific vulnerability\"**。TAWM 是强化 novelty 的最关键参考文献。\n13\t\n14\t## Venue\n15\t\n16\t**CoRL 2026** — Deadline: 2026-05-29\n17\t\n18\t## Narrative Positioning\n19\t\n20\tThis is a **diagnostic analysis paper**, not a method paper. We do not propose a new algorithm. Instead, we provide a systematic, controlled investigation answering: *Why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?*\n21\t\n22\tThe contribution is a **strongly supported causal chain** from mechanism to failure mode, backed by a factorial experiment design that isolates each factor, plus a **practical mitigation guideline** (restrict imagination to dt=1 during multi-dt training → 95% baseline recovery). Diagnostic papers are valued when they (a) address a widely-attempted but poorly-understood failure, (b) provide actionable insight for future method design, and (c) are methodologically rigorous.\n23\t\n24\tWe scope all claims to **DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model**. Generalizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## Core Claims\n29\t\n30\t### C1: Variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 consistently degrades performance\n31\t\n32\tTemporal abstraction via variable dt—whether learned or fixed—degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt=1 baseline across all tested configurations.\n33\t\n34\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n35\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n36\t| Baseline stability | exp_001_baseline (dt=1) | Stable convergence ~950 eval return |\n37\t| Fixed dt=2 collapse | exp_006_fixed_dt2 (dt=2, subsample ON, H=15) | Peak 485@35K → collapse to ~135@65K |\n38\t| Fixed dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 (dt=4) | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n39\t| Learned gate collapse | exp_005 (learned gate) | Peak 541@55K → 99@145K |\n40\t\n41\t**Strength**: Strong. Three independent dt>1 configurations all show degradation. No positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n42\t\n43\t### C2: Trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (>1.5x), creating high collapse risk\n44\t\n45\t**D032 update: nve>1.5x threshold has perfect separating power (4/4); collapse itself is stochastic.**\n46\t\n47\tThe trained dt_embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized v/e >1.5x baseline) across all tested seeds, but whether this overestimation progresses to catastrophic performance collapse is stochastic:\n48\t\n49\t- **WITH trained dt_emb**: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (008d seed 1: sustained 1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K → collapse; 009b seed 2: 1.62x@45K → self-correction to 0.38x@105K)\n50\t- **WITHOUT dt_emb (008f)**: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (seed 1 peak 1.02x@25K; seed 2 peak 1.26x@25K)\n51\t- **1.5x threshold (35K+)**: 4/4 perfect classification — reliable diagnostic marker separating trained vs untrained dt_emb\n52\t\n53\t**⚠️ 时间限定**: nve>1.5x 诊断适用于 **35K+ steps**（此时 baseline v/e 已充分收敛至 >0.24，比率稳定可靠）。25K 处 baseline v/e 仅 0.087，微小绝对差异可被放大为大比率（如 009b@25K nve=2.05x 和 008e_v3@25K nve=1.93x 均为 false positive）。论文中报告 threshold 时必须标注此限定。\n54\t\n55\t**Sustained vs transient overestimation**: The key difference between collapse (008d seed 1) and self-correction (009b seed 2) is the **temporal persistence** of nve>1.5x. Seed 1 maintained nve>1.5x across 3 consecutive checkpoints at 35K+ (1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K, accelerating), locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeded 1.5x at 45K (1.62x) but dropped to 1.41x@55K and continued declining, allowing self-correction. This suggests a \"point of no return\" where sustained overestimation becomes irreversible, with early training stochasticity (initialization, data sampling order) determining which path is taken (cf. Henderson et al. 2018, Bjorck et al. 2022, Qiao et al. 2023).\n56\t\n57\tThe trained dt_embedding enables precise dt prediction in imagination, but RSSM state prediction quality at dt>1 is insufficient to support this precision. The result is conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) that drives actor learning toward incorrect policies. Without dt_embedding conditioning, the overestimation feedback loop is blocked entirely (nve never exceeds 1.5x).\n58\t\n59\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n60\t|----------|-----------|------------|"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91b8143b-3ff1-42b2-89c4-d63e7f5bf555","uuid":"68998644-b611-4200-93e8-9d673f0f5a8b","timestamp":"2026-05-16T13:37:10.979Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T115400/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing: Diagnostic Analysis of Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL\n\n## Title Candidates\n\n1. **\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination\"**\n2. ~~**\"The Delusional Critic Problem in DreamerV3's Variable-Rate Imagination\"**~~ (deprecated — \"delusional critic\" conflicts with Lu et al. 2018)\n3. **\"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"** ← preferred after D037 terminology decision\n4. **\"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"**\n\n**⚠️ D037 术语决定**: \"delusional critic\" → **\"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"** (简称 CIVO)。Lu et al. 2018 的 delusional bias 指 policy-class constraints 导致不可实现的 Q 值，本文机制是 model imprecision + learned conditioning amplification，概念不同。Dream 模拟三档审稿一致指出术语误导。全文替换：\"delusional critic\" → \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"，可 cite Lu et al. 2018 as analogy but must explicitly differentiate。\n\n**⚠️ D037 TAWM 对比（关键 novelty 升级）**: TAWM (Nhu et al., ICML 2025) 在 TD-MPC2 (MPC setting) 中成功使用 dt-conditioning。本文发现 dt-conditioning 在 imagination-based actor-critic 中失败 → **novelty 从 \"known failure class 的 specific instance\" 升级为 \"dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination — here's the architecture-specific vulnerability\"**。TAWM 是强化 novelty 的最关键参考文献。\n\n## Venue\n\n**CoRL 2026** — Deadline: 2026-05-29\n\n## Narrative Positioning\n\nThis is a **diagnostic analysis paper**, not a method paper. We do not propose a new algorithm. Instead, we provide a systematic, controlled investigation answering: *Why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?*\n\nThe contribution is a **strongly supported causal chain** from mechanism to failure mode, backed by a factorial experiment design that isolates each factor, plus a **practical mitigation guideline** (restrict imagination to dt=1 during multi-dt training → 95% baseline recovery). Diagnostic papers are valued when they (a) address a widely-attempted but poorly-understood failure, (b) provide actionable insight for future method design, and (c) are methodologically rigorous.\n\nWe scope all claims to **DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model**. Generalizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n\n---\n\n## Core Claims\n\n### C1: Variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 consistently degrades performance\n\nTemporal abstraction via variable dt—whether learned or fixed—degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt=1 baseline across all tested configurations.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Baseline stability | exp_001_baseline (dt=1) | Stable convergence ~950 eval return |\n| Fixed dt=2 collapse | exp_006_fixed_dt2 (dt=2, subsample ON, H=15) | Peak 485@35K → collapse to ~135@65K |\n| Fixed dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 (dt=4) | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n| Learned gate collapse | exp_005 (learned gate) | Peak 541@55K → 99@145K |\n\n**Strength**: Strong. Three independent dt>1 configurations all show degradation. No positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n\n### C2: Trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (>1.5x), creating high collapse risk\n\n**D032 update: nve>1.5x threshold has perfect separating power (4/4); collapse itself is stochastic.**\n\nThe trained dt_embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized v/e >1.5x baseline) across all tested seeds, but whether this overestimation progresses to catastrophic performance collapse is stochastic:\n\n- **WITH trained dt_emb**: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (008d seed 1: sustained 1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K → collapse; 009b seed 2: 1.62x@45K → self-correction to 0.38x@105K)\n- **WITHOUT dt_emb (008f)**: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (seed 1 peak 1.02x@25K; seed 2 peak 1.26x@25K)\n- **1.5x threshold (35K+)**: 4/4 perfect classification — reliable diagnostic marker separating trained vs untrained dt_emb\n\n**⚠️ 时间限定**: nve>1.5x 诊断适用于 **35K+ steps**（此时 baseline v/e 已充分收敛至 >0.24，比率稳定可靠）。25K 处 baseline v/e 仅 0.087，微小绝对差异可被放大为大比率（如 009b@25K nve=2.05x 和 008e_v3@25K nve=1.93x 均为 false positive）。论文中报告 threshold 时必须标注此限定。\n\n**Sustained vs transient overestimation**: The key difference between collapse (008d seed 1) and self-correction (009b seed 2) is the **temporal persistence** of nve>1.5x. Seed 1 maintained nve>1.5x across 3 consecutive checkpoints at 35K+ (1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K, accelerating), locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeded 1.5x at 45K (1.62x) but dropped to 1.41x@55K and continued declining, allowing self-correction. This suggests a \"point of no return\" where sustained overestimation becomes irreversible, with early training stochasticity (initialization, data sampling order) determining which path is taken (cf. Henderson et al. 2018, Bjorck et al. 2022, Qiao et al. 2023).\n\nThe trained dt_embedding enables precise dt prediction in imagination, but RSSM state prediction quality at dt>1 is insufficient to support this precision. The result is conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) that drives actor learning toward incorrect policies. Without dt_embedding conditioning, the overestimation feedback loop is blocked entirely (nve never exceeds 1.5x).\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":314}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018E5Aa92pUJTucf1uCYNakc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have all the context needed. Generating the complete best-tier predictions for all 20 planned experiments.\n\n```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  CIVO is a general architectural vulnerability in imagination-based actor-critic with dt-conditioning.\n  Under best tier, the gradient decomposition (93% obs decoder → dt_emb, 7% KL, 0% reward) + Full-SGS\n  (exp_018, NVE=1.00x→0.72x) closes the mechanism at gradient level. Cross-3-environment replication\n  (Walker Walk ✓, Cheetah Run, PickCube/Cartpole) elevates the finding from DreamerV3-specific to\n  structural warning for temporal-abstraction-in-world-models. TAWM contrast (MPC succeeds, imagination\n  fails) is the headline novelty. The 1.5x NVE threshold maintains ≥3/4 separating power in every new\n  environment. Pathway B (008e_v3_s2-type eval-driven collapse) is either rare (≤1/4 cross-env) or gets\n  clean mechanistic characterization via control experiment. Missing from current plan: Full-SGS\n  replication in Cheetah/Cartpole — needed to close \"gradient mechanism generalizes\" claim per tier spec.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Walker Walk — Multi-seed replication\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: >\n      Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds. Registry already has data:\n      009e (seed 1) collapsed NVE peak 4.12x; 009b (seed 2) self-corrected NVE peak 2.05x.\n      Under best tier, both seeds exceed NVE>1.5x (confirmed), collapse is stochastic 1/2 (confirmed).\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 131\n      seed1_nve_peak: 4.12\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n      seed2_eval_105K: 268\n      seed2_nve_peak: 2.05\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction_nve_105K: 0.38\n      both_seeds_exceed_1.5x: true\n      threshold_separating_power: \"2/2 exceed 1.5x at 35K+\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2 (stochastic)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trained dt_emb creates systematic overestimation because the RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2\n      (obs gap 1.30x at inflated absolute levels, reward gap 2.78x) is amplified through the\n      observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient). Every seed with trained\n      dt_emb reliably enters the pathological NVE>1.5x regime because the gradient mechanism is\n      deterministic — the dt_emb learns to differentiate dt values, and the critic exploits this\n      differentiation against inaccurate RSSM predictions. Whether NVE>1.5x becomes sustained (→collapse)\n      or transient (→self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity: replay buffer sampling\n      order, initialization, and whether the actor's policy gradient from real returns can counteract\n      the critic's overestimate before it locks in (primacy bias, Qiao et al. 2023).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"NVE trajectory comparison (seed 1 vs seed 2)\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_nve, seed1_eval, seed2_nve, seed2_eval]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.78, 167, 2.05, 141]\n          - [35000, 1.71, 146, 1.24, 164]\n          - [45000, 2.09, 141, 1.62, 141]\n          - [55000, 4.12, 83, 1.41, 172]\n          - [65000, 1.23, 250, 0.94, 209]\n          - [75000, 1.48, 178, 0.66, 197]\n          - [85000, 1.15, 202, 0.51, 236]\n          - [95000, 1.73, 133, 0.43, 279]\n          - [105000, 1.57, 131, 0.38, 268]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE time series: seed 1 sustained >1.5x (35-55K) → collapse; seed 2 transient >1.5x (25-45K) → self-correction. Shaded 1.5x threshold line.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Actual data confirms best-tier predictions. 2/2 trained dt_emb seeds exceed NVE 1.5x,\n      maintaining perfect threshold separating power (now 4/4 across trained/untrained combined).\n      Collapse stochasticity (1/2) is a feature of the narrative: trained dt_emb is a necessary\n      condition for CIVO, not sufficient for collapse. The sustained-vs-transient NVE persistence\n      distinguishes the two outcomes.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_012\n    description: >\n      Replicate causal control stability. Seed 1 (009f) was killed (infra). Seed 2 (009c)\n      ran to 105K: NVE peaked 1.26x, eval declined to 148. Under best tier, seed 1 re-run\n      shows NVE<1.5x; seed 2 extended to 200K plateaus without crossing 1.5x.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_rerun_eval_105K: 420\n      seed1_rerun_nve_peak: 0.95\n      seed1_rerun_nve_105K: 0.35\n      seed1_rerun_collapse: false\n      seed2_eval_105K: 148\n      seed2_nve_105K: 1.14\n      seed2_eval_200K: 280\n      seed2_nve_200K: 0.85\n      seed2_nve_peak_ever: 1.26\n      seed2_crosses_1.5x: false\n      both_seeds_below_1.5x_at_35K_plus: true\n      threshold_separating_power: \"0/2 exceed 1.5x\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without dt_embedding (dim=0), the critic receives no dt-conditioning information.\n      The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1 from dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 2.78x\n      with trained dt_emb), so the systematic overestimation feedback loop cannot form. Seed 2's\n      rising NVE at 105K (1.14x) reflects value inertia — value_mean was still rising (111.2@105K)\n      while eval declined, but this is fundamentally different from CIVO: the value is simply\n      slow to track a declining eval, not systematically overestimating. Extended to 200K, the\n      value function adjusts downward as it accumulates more accurate return estimates, pulling\n      NVE back below 1.0x. Seed 1 re-run (with stable infrastructure) follows the zigzag\n      recovery pattern of 008f seed 0 (503@105K), because the underlying mechanism is the\n      same — CIVO pathway is structurally blocked.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Seed 2 extended trajectory (predicted 105K-200K)\"\n        columns: [step, eval_return, nve, note]\n        rows:\n          - [105000, 148, 1.14, \"actual\"]\n          - [125000, 195, 1.05, \"value still adjusting\"]\n          - [145000, 240, 0.92, \"value correction begins\"]\n          - [165000, 270, 0.88, \"eval recovering\"]\n          - [185000, 290, 0.85, \"stabilizing\"]\n          - [200000, 280, 0.85, \"plateau\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE trajectory for both seeds with 1.5x threshold line. Demonstrates NVE never crosses 1.5x, contrasting with 008d seeds.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Seed 2's decline after 75K is concerning but does NOT constitute CIVO — NVE never exceeded\n      1.5x, and the value dynamics are qualitatively different (slow value tracking vs value explosion).\n      The 200K extension confirms plateau without threshold crossing. Seed 1 re-run expected to\n      show healthier zigzag recovery (like seed 0), improving cross-seed consistency. Critical for\n      C2: 0/2 untrained seeds cross 1.5x, maintaining 4/4→6/6 threshold separation when combined\n      with plan_011.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_014\n    description: >\n      Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination mitigation. Already done: seed 1 success\n      (869.9@105K), seed 2 collapsed (137@105K) via non-CIVO Pathway B. Under best tier,\n      Pathway B gets clean mechanistic characterization.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 869.9\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.80\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_eval_105K: 137\n      seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n      seed2_collapse: true\n      seed2_pathway: \"B (eval-driven, entropy↑)\"\n      seed2_actor_entropy_collapse: -5.21_to_-3.83\n      pathway_b_rate_walker: \"1/2\"\n      pathway_a_blocked: true\n      gate_dt_mean_both_seeds: 1.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      dt=1 imagination blocks Pathway A (classic CIVO) because the critic never evaluates dt>1\n      imagination steps — the value overestimation feedback loop requires dt>1 predictions where\n      RSSM fidelity degrades. However, the trained dt_embedding still receives gradient from\n      observation decoders during world model training on real dt>1 transitions (subsample ON includes\n      dt=2 in training data). This creates a secondary vulnerability: the dt_emb encodes dt-specific\n      information that subtly corrupts the latent state representation, even when imagination uses only\n      dt=1. Seed 2's collapse shows eval-driven destabilization (715→452→137) with increasing entropy\n      (-5.21→-3.83), the opposite signature of CIVO (which has decreasing entropy). The policy\n      destabilizes because the world model's latent space has been distorted by the trained dt_emb,\n      not because the critic directly overestimates. This Pathway B is stochastic — seed 1 avoids it\n      because its specific initialization leads to a latent representation that is less corrupted by\n      the dt_emb gradients.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\"\n        columns: [feature, pathway_a_008d_s1, pathway_b_008e_v3_s2]\n        rows:\n          - [\"value change at collapse\", \"+130% explosion\", \"+13% mild\"]\n          - [\"eval change at collapse\", \"-43% follows value\", \"-81% leads\"]\n          - [\"NVE driver\", \"value numerator\", \"eval denominator\"]\n          - [\"actor_entropy\", \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88) deterministic\", \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83) random\"]\n          - [\"mechanism\", \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\", \"eval crash → value lag\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-panel: seed 1 healthy trajectory vs seed 2 Pathway B collapse, with entropy overlay showing opposite signatures.\"\n    analysis: >\n      C6 is partially falsified but transformed into a stronger finding: trained dt_embedding\n      creates vulnerability through MULTIPLE pathways. dt=1 imagination eliminates the more\n      understood Pathway A but exposes the previously hidden Pathway B. Under best tier, Pathway B\n      is characterized cleanly enough that it becomes a contribution rather than a weakness — the\n      paper demonstrates that surface-level mitigations are insufficient.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_013\n    description: >\n      Replicate value growth suppression and V-recovery pattern with 2 additional seeds.\n      Seed 0 showed 778@105K (85% baseline), NVE=0.59x with two dip-recovery cycles.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 720\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.90]\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n      seed1_v_recovery_count: 2\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_eval_105K: 690\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.50, 0.95]\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.58\n      seed2_v_recovery_count: 1\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 729\n      cross_seed_mean_nve_105K: 0.60\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-20% (range -15% to -25%)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF, so dt_emb trains but never sees dt>1 data) means the\n      embedding learns from dt=1 transitions only. When imagination encounters dt>1 predictions,\n      the dt_emb provides random-equivalent conditioning for those values. The critic cannot form\n      dt-specific overestimates because the dt_emb carries no learned dt>1 information.\n      NVE stays structurally suppressed (0.5-0.9x) because value growth is bounded by the\n      quality of dt=1-only imagination. The V-recovery pattern (zigzag) arises from periodic\n      exploration-exploitation oscillation in a regime where the critic is conservative.\n      New seeds show similar pattern with ±10% variance in eval (driven by early exploration\n      stochasticity), but the NVE suppression is robust because it's a structural property\n      of the unexposed dt_emb, not a stochastic outcome.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-seed V-recovery summary\"\n        columns: [seed, eval_55K, eval_65K, eval_85K, eval_105K, nve_55K, nve_105K]\n        rows:\n          - [0, 488, 608, 584, 778, 0.93, 0.59]\n          - [1, 450, 570, 620, 720, 0.85, 0.62]\n          - [2, 420, 540, 560, 690, 0.90, 0.58]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"3-seed learning curves with confidence bands, showing V-recovery pattern reproducibility. NVE overlay stays below 1.0x throughout.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms C3 robustly: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection. The ±10% eval\n      variance across seeds is well within DreamerV3's typical seed variability. The key\n      invariant — NVE never exceeds 1.0x at 35K+ — holds across all seeds, consistent with\n      the gradient-level explanation (dt_emb unexposed to dt>1 data → no dt>1-specific\n      gradient signal → no systematic overestimation).\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\"\n    plan_id: plan_015\n    description: >\n      Already run as exp_015b. NVE 0.39-0.47 (far below 1.0), actor_loss→0 (gradient starvation),\n      eval volatile 80-238. Under best tier, this confirms dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition\n      for CIVO, with frozen dt_emb revealing a distinct failure mode (gradient starvation ≠ CIVO).\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 204\n      nve_range: [0.39, 0.47]\n      nve_105K: 0.42\n      actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n      actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n      collapse: false\n      civo_detected: false\n      gradient_starvation: true\n      gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Frozen dt_emb (requires_grad=False, random init) provides constant random conditioning\n      per dt value. The critic cannot learn dt-specific value adjustments → NVE stays far below\n      1.0x (0.39-0.47), confirming CIVO requires TRAINING of dt_emb. However, the frozen random\n      embedding injects constant noise into the actor-critic system: the actor receives\n      confusing value gradients (actor_loss→0 = gradient signal vanishes), and the gate locks\n      to dt=2 100% because the gate network never receives informative gradients to adjust.\n      This produces a distinct failure mode — not value overestimation (CIVO) but gradient\n      starvation from persistent noise injection. The NVE being far below 1.0x (vs >1.5x for\n      trained) is the clean separation: frozen dt_emb → NO CIVO pathway is possible.\n      This completes the 2×2 ablation matrix: trained+present (008d)→CIVO, trained+absent (008f)→no CIVO,\n      frozen+present (015b)→no CIVO but gradient starvation, confirming dt_emb TRAINING is the\n      necessary condition.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"2×2 dt_emb ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H=8)\"\n        columns: [config, dt_emb_status, nve_peak_35K_plus, collapse, failure_mode]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d\", \"trained (grad ON)\", \"4.12x\", \"yes (1/2)\", \"CIVO\"]\n          - [\"008f\", \"absent (dim=0)\", \"1.26x\", \"no\", \"reward degradation\"]\n          - [\"015b\", \"frozen (grad OFF)\", \"0.47x\", \"no\", \"gradient starvation\"]\n          - [\"008c\", \"untrained (subsample OFF)\", \"0.90x\", \"no\", \"learning signal reduction\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"2×2 matrix visualization: NVE heatmap showing only trained+present exceeds 1.5x. Side annotation of distinct failure modes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      015b closes the \"is dt_emb TRAINING necessary?\" question definitively. The 2×2 matrix\n      shows that only trained dt_emb with gradient flow produces NVE>1.5x. All other\n      configurations avoid CIVO but have their own distinct degradation mechanisms — this is\n      the nuanced picture that elevates the paper from \"trained dt_emb is bad\" to \"the gradient\n      pathway through observation decoders→dt_emb is the specific vulnerability.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\"\n    plan_id: plan_016\n    description: >\n      Additional seeds to narrow Pathway B collapse probability. Current rate: 1/2 (seed 1 ok,\n      seed 2 collapsed). Under best tier, Pathway B is rare (≤1/4 cross-env).\n    expected_results:\n      seed3_eval_105K: 840\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.78\n      seed3_collapse: false\n      seed4_eval_105K: 810\n      seed4_nve_105K: 0.82\n      seed4_collapse: false\n      pathway_b_rate_updated: \"1/4 (25%)\"\n      pathway_b_rare_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Under best tier, Pathway B is a rare event. With seeds 3-4 both succeeding, the\n      rate drops from 1/2 to 1/4, consistent with the tier hypothesis that Pathway B is\n      ≤1/4 frequency. The rationale: Pathway B requires a specific conjunction of early\n      training conditions — the trained dt_emb must corrupt the latent representation in a\n      way that destabilizes the actor without triggering the classic CIVO value explosion.\n      This is a narrow failure mode that occurs when the dt_emb gradient from observation\n      decoders creates a latent-space distortion that specifically disrupts the actor's policy\n      gradient, which depends on seed-specific replay buffer composition. Most seeds avoid\n      this because the distortion is mild enough that the actor adapts. The 1/4 rate makes\n      Pathway B a secondary finding — acknowledged and characterized, but not the dominant\n      failure mode (CIVO, Pathway A, remains primary).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"008e_v3 cross-seed summary (4 seeds)\"\n        columns: [seed, eval_105K, nve_105K, collapse, pathway]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 869.9, 0.80, false, \"-\"]\n          - [2, 137, 3.78, true, \"B (eval-driven)\"]\n          - [3, 840, 0.78, false, \"-\"]\n          - [4, 810, 0.82, false, \"-\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"4-seed learning curves with seed 2 collapse highlighted. IQM + 95% CI excluding collapsed seed vs including.\"\n    analysis: >\n      1/4 Pathway B rate is manageable for the narrative: \"dt=1 imagination eliminates the\n      primary CIVO pathway in 4/4 seeds; a secondary eval-driven instability affects 1/4 seeds,\n      characterized by increasing entropy rather than value explosion.\" This positions dt=1\n      imagination as a partial mitigation that eliminates the understood mechanism while leaving\n      a residual vulnerability from the trained dt_emb's latent-space corruption.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Cheetah Run — Cross-environment replication\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_020\n    description: >\n      dt=1 baseline in second DMC environment. Seed 1 (010a) already done: peak 849.8.\n      Under best tier, 3 seeds establish clean reference at ~800-850.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_peak: 849.8\n      seed1_eval_500K: 849.8\n      seed2_eval_peak: 830\n      seed2_eval_500K: 815\n      seed3_eval_peak: 860\n      seed3_eval_500K: 840\n      cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n      cross_seed_std: 15\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.22\n      ve_ratio_65K: 0.50\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.62\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~0.65-0.70 from 135K+\"\n      convergence_step: 120000\n    result_rationale: >\n      DreamerV3 is well-calibrated for Cheetah Run in the original paper. The RSSM handles\n      Cheetah's 17-dim proprioceptive observation well. Baseline v/e calibration should be\n      similar to Walker Walk (~0.65-0.70 at convergence) because the value function learns\n      accurate return estimates when the world model is accurate (dt=1 only). The slightly\n      lower peak than Walker Walk (850 vs 950) reflects Cheetah Run's harder reward landscape\n      (requires coordinated leg movements for running gait). Seed variance ±15 is typical\n      for DreamerV3 on DMC locomotion tasks.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline v/e calibration\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_ve, seed2_ve, seed3_ve, mean_ve]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 0.09, 0.10, 0.08, 0.09]\n          - [35000, 0.22, 0.24, 0.21, 0.22]\n          - [55000, 0.38, 0.40, 0.37, 0.38]\n          - [75000, 0.55, 0.52, 0.56, 0.54]\n          - [105000, 0.62, 0.60, 0.64, 0.62]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed learning curves with IQM and 95% CI bands.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Clean baseline establishes reference for cross-environment CIVO replication. The v/e\n      calibration at ~0.22@35K provides the denominator for NVE threshold calculations in\n      Cheetah Run experiments.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_021\n    description: >\n      Core cross-environment CIVO replication. exp_010b is RUNNING. Under best tier,\n      NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ with collapse in 1-2/3 seeds.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_peak: 380\n      seed1_eval_105K: 95\n      seed1_nve_35K: 1.85\n      seed1_nve_45K: 2.40\n      seed1_nve_55K: 3.10\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n      seed2_eval_peak: 420\n      seed2_eval_105K: 310\n      seed2_nve_35K: 1.70\n      seed2_nve_45K: 1.55\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.45\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      seed3_eval_peak: 350\n      seed3_eval_105K: 120\n      seed3_nve_35K: 1.90\n      seed3_nve_55K: 2.80\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      cross_seed_nve_above_1.5x_count: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n      cheetah_civo_nve_35K_plus_range: [1.70, 3.10]\n    result_rationale: >\n      The CIVO mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), not task-specific.\n      The same gradient pathway (93% obs decoder→dt_emb) operates in Cheetah Run because the\n      RSSM architecture is identical — only the observation dimensionality differs (17 vs 24 dims).\n      The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 generalizes: Cheetah Run's running dynamics involve rapid\n      velocity changes that are poorly predicted at dt=2 steps, creating the same obs_MSE gap\n      that drives CIVO in Walker Walk. NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ is expected because the v/e\n      calibration baseline is similar (~0.22@35K), and the trained dt_emb introduces the same\n      systematic bias. Collapse rate 2/3 (within best tier range [0.33, 0.67]) because Cheetah's\n      dynamics may be slightly more chaotic, making the self-correction pathway harder to find.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_nve, seed2_nve, seed3_nve, threshold]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.55, 1.40, 1.60, 1.5]\n          - [35000, 1.85, 1.70, 1.90, 1.5]\n          - [45000, 2.40, 1.55, 2.30, 1.5]\n          - [55000, 3.10, 1.20, 2.80, 1.5]\n          - [65000, 3.50, 0.90, 3.20, 1.5]\n          - [75000, null, 0.70, null, 1.5]\n          - [105000, null, 0.45, null, 1.5]\n      - name: \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\"\n        columns: [environment, seeds_above_1.5x, collapse_rate, nve_range_35K_plus]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", \"2/2\", \"1/2\", \"[1.62, 4.12]\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", \"3/3\", \"2/3\", \"[1.70, 3.10]\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-environment NVE comparison panel: Walker Walk (left) vs Cheetah Run (right), with 1.5x threshold. Shows mechanism generalizes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the critical experiment for the paper. 3/3 seeds exceeding NVE 1.5x in a second\n      environment elevates the finding from \"Walker Walk specific\" to \"RSSM architectural\n      vulnerability.\" The NVE range [1.70, 3.10] falls within the tier-specified [1.6, 3.5].\n      Combined with Walker Walk, the 1.5x threshold now has 5/5 separating power across\n      environments for the \"trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x\" claim.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_022\n    description: >\n      Cross-environment causal control. Under best tier, NVE 0.3-0.9x, no CIVO.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 450\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.40\n      seed2_eval_105K: 380\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.55\n      seed3_eval_105K: 420\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 417\n      cross_seed_nve_range: [0.35, 0.60]\n      seeds_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      collapse_count: 0\n      reward_mae_ratio_vs_baseline: \"~70x\"\n      cheetah_no_dt_emb_nve_35K_plus: [0.35, 0.60]\n    result_rationale: >\n      Removing dt_embedding in Cheetah Run has the same effect as in Walker Walk: the critic\n      cannot exploit dt-conditioned predictions. The reward head loses dt-discriminative power\n      (reward gap collapses to ~1.0x), and NVE stays within normal range because the value\n      function can only track actual return magnitudes, not inflated dt-specific estimates.\n      The absolute eval is lower than baseline (~50% of 850) due to reward prediction degradation\n      (MAE inflated ~70x) — same trade-off as Walker Walk's 008f. NVE range [0.35, 0.60] is\n      within the tier-specified [0.3, 0.9]. The 0/3 result maintains the threshold's cross-env\n      separating power.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cheetah Run no-dt_emb causal control\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_nve, seed2_nve, seed3_nve]\n        rows:\n          - [35000, 0.80, 0.85, 0.78]\n          - [55000, 0.60, 0.70, 0.65]\n          - [75000, 0.45, 0.58, 0.50]\n          - [105000, 0.40, 0.55, 0.45]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run causal control NVE: all 3 seeds stay well below 1.5x threshold.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Combined with plan_021, the cross-environment causal control is complete: 3/3 trained\n      dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/3 no-dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x → 6/6 perfect classification\n      in Cheetah Run. Across Walker Walk + Cheetah Run: 5/5 trained exceed, 0/5 untrained\n      exceed → 10/10 perfect threshold classification. Cross-env threshold accuracy = 1.0,\n      well within tier spec [0.83, 1.0].\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_023\n    description: >\n      V-recovery pattern replication in Cheetah Run. Under best tier, NVE<1.0x with\n      near-baseline recovery.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 710\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.55\n      seed2_eval_105K: 680\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.60\n      seed3_eval_105K: 740\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.52\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 710\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-16%\"\n      nve_range: [0.50, 0.65]\n      v_recovery_pattern: true\n      collapse_count: 0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Same mechanism as Walker Walk 008a: untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → no dt>1 in training\n      data) blocks the CIVO gradient pathway. The dt_emb trains from dt=1 transitions only,\n      providing no dt>1-specific information to the critic. NVE is structurally suppressed\n      below 1.0x. The V-recovery pattern generalizes because it's driven by the exploration-\n      exploitation oscillation in a conservative-critic regime, which is architecture-dependent\n      (RSSM), not task-dependent. Eval at ~84% baseline (710/850) is consistent with Walker\n      Walk's 85% (778/916).\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run 008a-equivalent: 3-seed V-recovery curves with NVE overlay.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms C3 cross-environment: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection in\n      both Walker Walk and Cheetah Run, with consistent NVE suppression (0.5-0.6x) and\n      eval recovery to ~85% baseline. The mechanism (unexposed dt_emb → no dt>1 gradient\n      → no systematic overestimation) is environment-agnostic.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Cartpole Swingup — Third environment\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_050\n    description: >\n      dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment. Broadens scope beyond locomotion\n      to test whether CIVO depends on movement dynamics.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_peak: 845\n      seed2_eval_peak: 860\n      seed3_eval_peak: 835\n      cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n      cross_seed_std: 13\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.23\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.60\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~0.63-0.68\"\n      convergence_step: 100000\n    result_rationale: >\n      DreamerV3 handles Cartpole Swingup well — 5-dim observation and 1-dim action make it a\n      simpler learning problem than locomotion. The RSSM achieves good prediction quality\n      rapidly. Baseline peaks at ~840-860 with tight cross-seed variance (~±13) because the\n      task has lower stochasticity than locomotion. The v/e calibration (~0.23@35K, ~0.63@convergence)\n      provides the reference for NVE calculations.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole Swingup baseline learning curves with v/e calibration.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Establishes clean reference. Cartpole's simpler observation space (5-dim) tests whether\n      CIVO still occurs when the RSSM has less room for fidelity degradation — the key question\n      is whether even small absolute errors in dt>1 prediction, amplified by trained dt_emb, are\n      sufficient to trigger NVE>1.5x.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_051\n    description: >\n      CIVO trigger in non-locomotion environment. Under best tier, NVE>1.5x in ≥2/3 seeds.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_35K: 1.65\n      seed1_nve_55K: 2.20\n      seed1_eval_105K: 150\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed2_nve_35K: 1.55\n      seed2_nve_55K: 1.40\n      seed2_eval_105K: 380\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed3_nve_35K: 1.75\n      seed3_nve_55K: 2.50\n      seed3_eval_105K: 120\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      seeds_above_1.5x_35K_plus: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Even with Cartpole's simpler 5-dim observation, the RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is\n      sufficient to trigger CIVO. The mechanism operates through the observation decoder→dt_emb\n      gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient), not through observation dimensionality per se.\n      A 5-dim observation still has enough structure for the dt_emb to learn dt-specific\n      representations that the RSSM can't support. The pole angle and angular velocity components\n      are particularly sensitive to temporal skipping — Cartpole dynamics are chaotic near the\n      inverted position, so dt=2 predictions of these components degrade substantially.\n      NVE>1.5x in 3/3 seeds maintains the perfect threshold separating power. Collapse in 2/3\n      is within the tier-specified range.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-environment CIVO summary (3 envs)\"\n        columns: [environment, obs_dim, seeds_above_1.5x, collapse_rate]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", 24, \"2/2\", \"1/2\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", 17, \"3/3\", \"2/3\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole Swingup\", 5, \"3/3\", \"2/3\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"3-environment CIVO comparison: NVE trajectories across Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CIVO triggers in 3/3 environments regardless of observation dimensionality. This is\n      the strongest evidence that CIVO is an architectural vulnerability of RSSM + trained\n      dt_emb, not a task-specific phenomenon. environments_with_confirmed_civo = 3, matching\n      the tier specification.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_052\n    description: >\n      Causal control replication in third environment.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.50\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.60\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n      seeds_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 480\n      collapse_count: 0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Same causal mechanism as Walker Walk and Cheetah Run: no dt_embedding → no dt-conditioned\n      value exploitation → NVE stays within normal range. The result is expected to be robust\n      because the causal pathway (trained dt_emb → observation decoder gradient → systematic\n      overestimation) is completely removed by setting dt_embed_dim=0. Cross-environment\n      consistency of this control strengthens the causal claim from \"observed in one env\"\n      to \"architecturally guaranteed.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole Swingup causal control: NVE well below 1.5x for all seeds.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Across 3 environments: trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x in 8/8 seeds; no dt_emb → NVE<1.5x\n      in 0/8 seeds. Threshold classification accuracy = 16/16 = 1.0. This is the paper's\n      strongest quantitative result.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Analysis experiments (0-1 GPU hour)\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\"\n    plan_id: plan_030\n    description: >\n      Already completed (exp_rssm_fidelity_analysis). Under best tier, results provide\n      the mechanistic foundation showing RSSM fidelity gap drives CIVO.\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_obs_gap: 2.04\n      baseline_reward_gap: 1.56\n      008d_obs_gap: 1.30\n      008d_obs_dt1_absolute: 41.31\n      008d_obs_dt2_absolute: 53.67\n      008d_reward_gap: 2.78\n      008f_obs_gap: 2.75\n      008f_reward_gap: 1.01\n      008d_dt1_obs_vs_baseline: \"3.06x (41.31 / 13.49)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The RSSM fidelity analysis reveals the root cause: dt=2 predictions are systematically\n      worse than dt=1 across all models. The trained dt_emb model (008d) shows paradoxically\n      low obs_gap ratio (1.30x) because its dt=1 absolute error is already 3x baseline (model\n      has degraded overall). The reward gap (2.78x) in 008d is the smoking gun — the trained\n      dt_emb enables the reward head to make dt-specific predictions, but these predictions are\n      systematically biased at dt=2 due to the underlying RSSM fidelity gap. In 008f (no dt_emb),\n      reward gap collapses to 1.01x — the reward head treats dt=1 and dt=2 identically, preventing\n      systematic bias. This explains why 008f avoids CIVO despite higher absolute reward MAE.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"RSSM Fidelity (absolute + ratio)\"\n        columns: [model, dt1_obs_MSE, dt2_obs_MSE, obs_gap, dt1_reward_MAE, dt2_reward_MAE, reward_gap]\n        rows:\n          - [\"baseline\", 13.49, 27.50, \"2.04x\", 0.0102, 0.0159, \"1.56x\"]\n          - [\"008d (trained)\", 41.31, 53.67, \"1.30x\", 0.0568, 0.1579, \"2.78x\"]\n          - [\"008f (no dt_emb)\", 8.01, 22.04, \"2.75x\", 0.7469, 0.7526, \"1.01x\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: absolute obs_MSE and reward_MAE at dt=1 vs dt=2 for three models, with gap ratio annotated.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Must present absolute values AND ratios side-by-side. 008d's 1.30x obs gap is misleading\n      without the absolute context (41.31 at dt=1 = 3x baseline). The reward gap (2.78x)\n      directly explains CIVO: trained dt_emb creates dt-specific reward biases that the\n      critic amplifies through the imagination rollout.\n\n  - name: \"Actor entropy analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_031\n    description: >\n      0 GPU cost — extract from existing metrics.jsonl logs. Separates CIVO (Pathway A)\n      from non-CIVO (Pathway B) collapse signatures.\n    expected_results:\n      008d_s1_entropy_35K: -3.47\n      008d_s1_entropy_55K: -4.88\n      008d_s1_entropy_direction: \"decreasing (policy → deterministic)\"\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_direction: \"increasing (policy → random)\"\n      008a_entropy_stable_range: \"[-4.5, -3.8]\"\n      008c_entropy_stable_range: \"[-3.5, -2.8]\"\n      baseline_entropy_converged: -5.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Actor entropy is the cleanest discriminator between Pathway A and Pathway B. In classic\n      CIVO (Pathway A, 008d), the overestimating critic provides strong gradients toward a\n      narrow set of actions → entropy decreases as the policy becomes overconfident in\n      the wrong actions. In Pathway B (008e_v3_s2), the policy destabilizes from latent-space\n      corruption → entropy increases as the actor loses confidence and explores randomly.\n      Healthy configs (008a, 008c) maintain stable entropy in a moderate range, reflecting\n      properly calibrated exploration-exploitation. The baseline converges to low entropy\n      (~-5.2) reflecting a well-learned deterministic policy, which is the correct behavior\n      for Walker Walk.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Actor entropy at collapse/stability\"\n        columns: [config, entropy_at_collapse_onset, entropy_direction, pathway]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d s1 (CIVO)\", -3.47, \"↓ to -4.88\", \"A\"]\n          - [\"008e_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)\", -5.21, \"↑ to -3.83\", \"B\"]\n          - [\"008a (healthy)\", -4.2, \"stable ±0.3\", \"-\"]\n          - [\"baseline\", -5.0, \"stable ±0.2\", \"-\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Actor entropy time series for collapsed vs healthy configs. Divergent arrows at collapse onset distinguish Pathway A (↓) from B (↑).\"\n    analysis: >\n      This figure is essential for the dual-pathway narrative. It provides the most intuitive\n      evidence that 008d and 008e_v3_s2 fail through different mechanisms, justifying the\n      Pathway A / Pathway B framing.\n\n  - name: \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\"\n    plan_id: plan_032\n    description: >\n      1 GPU hour — load checkpoints and evaluate. Shows distribution-level evidence\n      for CIVO beyond the scalar v/e ratio.\n    expected_results:\n      008d_35K_value_mean: 60.2\n      008d_35K_return_mean: 146.3\n      008d_35K_value_skewness: 1.8\n      008d_55K_value_mean: 137.9\n      008d_55K_return_mean: 82.8\n      008d_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.85\n      008a_55K_value_mean: 183.5\n      008a_55K_return_mean: 487.9\n      008a_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.35\n      baseline_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.40\n    result_rationale: >\n      The scalar v/e ratio is a proxy; distribution plots show where the divergence occurs.\n      In 008d at 55K (pre-collapse), the value distribution is right-skewed with 85% of\n      value predictions exceeding realized returns — a systematic overestimation signature.\n      In 008a (healthy, untrained dt_emb), the value distribution is symmetric around returns\n      with only 35% exceeding (slight underestimation, consistent with conservative critic).\n      The distribution shape directly shows the CIVO mechanism: trained dt_emb creates a\n      population of states where the critic is systematically optimistic, particularly for\n      states that would be reached via dt>1 imagination.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"2×2 panel: V(s) vs MC-return scatter for 008d/008a at 35K/55K. Diagonal = perfect calibration. 008d@55K shows systematic departure above diagonal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Addresses R3 (v/e is crude proxy) directly. The distribution plots provide a more\n      nuanced view: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate all values — it specifically affects\n      states reachable via dt>1 imagination, creating a bimodal distribution.\n\n  - name: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_033\n    description: >\n      0 GPU cost — extract dt_emb weights from checkpoints. Shows how dt=1 and dt=2\n      embeddings diverge during training.\n    expected_results:\n      008d_cosine_sim_5K: 0.92\n      008d_cosine_sim_25K: 0.65\n      008d_cosine_sim_35K: 0.42\n      008d_cosine_sim_55K: 0.28\n      008a_cosine_sim_55K: 0.88\n      008f_cosine_sim: \"N/A (no dt_emb)\"\n      015b_cosine_sim: 0.92\n      baseline_cosine_sim: \"N/A (no dt_emb)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trained dt_emb (008d) starts with similar dt=1/dt=2 embeddings (cosine ~0.92 from\n      random init) and diverges rapidly as the observation decoder gradient differentiates\n      them. By 35K (when NVE first exceeds 1.5x), cosine similarity has dropped to ~0.42 —\n      the embeddings are now substantially different, enabling the critic to make highly\n      dt-specific (and wrong) predictions. In 008a (subsample OFF, untrained on dt>1 data),\n      cosine similarity stays high (~0.88) because the gradient signal only comes from dt=1\n      transitions, providing no pressure to differentiate. In 015b (frozen), cosine similarity\n      stays at initialization (~0.92). The divergence timeline correlates with NVE onset,\n      providing indirect evidence that embedding differentiation drives CIVO.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"dt_emb cosine similarity evolution\"\n        columns: [step, \"008d (trained)\", \"008a (unexposed)\", \"015b (frozen)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [5000, 0.92, 0.92, 0.92]\n          - [15000, 0.80, 0.90, 0.92]\n          - [25000, 0.65, 0.89, 0.92]\n          - [35000, 0.42, 0.88, 0.92]\n          - [55000, 0.28, 0.88, 0.92]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cosine similarity decay plot with NVE threshold crossing annotated on the 008d curve.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Provides a mechanistic visualization of WHY trained dt_emb creates the vulnerability:\n      gradient-driven embedding divergence → dt-specific critic predictions → CIVO.\n      The correlation between divergence and NVE onset is compelling but not causal proof\n      by itself — it complements the gradient decomposition (D061) and Full-SGS (018) evidence.\n\n  - name: \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_034\n    description: >\n      0 GPU cost — computed from existing metrics. Tests robustness of 1.5x threshold\n      across all available data.\n    expected_results:\n      threshold_1.3x_accuracy: 0.75\n      threshold_1.3x_false_positives: 2\n      threshold_1.5x_accuracy: 1.0\n      threshold_1.5x_false_positives: 0\n      threshold_1.5x_false_negatives: 0\n      threshold_1.7x_accuracy: 0.875\n      threshold_1.7x_false_negatives: 1\n      threshold_2.0x_accuracy: 0.75\n      threshold_2.0x_false_negatives: 2\n      auroc_walker_walk: 1.0\n      optimal_threshold: 1.5\n      cross_env_threshold_accuracy: 1.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 1.5x, the current Walker Walk data has 4/4 perfect classification (2/2 trained above,\n      2/2 untrained below). At 1.3x, two untrained seeds (009c@105K=1.14x, 008a somewhere near\n      1.0x) could create false positives if the window is shifted earlier. At 1.7x, 009b's\n      peak of 1.62x@45K would be missed (false negative). At 2.0x, both 009b (2.05x@25K but\n      this is pre-35K) and potentially 008d at 35K (1.71x) would be missed. The 1.5x threshold\n      at 35K+ is the optimal operating point because: (a) baseline v/e has converged sufficiently\n      by 35K (>0.24, making ratios reliable), (b) 1.5x is approximately 2σ above baseline v/e\n      natural variation, and (c) it achieves perfect sensitivity and specificity in the current\n      data. Cross-environment accuracy stays at 1.0 under best tier because the mechanism is\n      architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), so the threshold generalizes.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Threshold sensitivity (Walker Walk, 35K+ window)\"\n        columns: [threshold, TP, FP, TN, FN, accuracy, notes]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1.3x\", 2, 1, 1, 0, \"3/4=0.75\", \"009c@105K=1.14x → FP edge case\"]\n          - [\"1.5x\", 2, 0, 2, 0, \"4/4=1.00\", \"optimal\"]\n          - [\"1.7x\", 1, 0, 2, 1, \"3/4=0.75\", \"009b peak 1.62x missed\"]\n          - [\"2.0x\", 1, 0, 2, 1, \"3/4=0.75\", \"only sustained CIVO detected\"]\n      - name: \"Cross-environment threshold accuracy (best tier, 1.5x)\"\n        columns: [environment, trained_above, untrained_below, accuracy]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", \"2/2\", \"2/2\", \"4/4\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", \"3/3\", \"3/3\", \"6/6\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole Swingup\", \"3/3\", \"3/3\", \"6/6\"]\n          - [\"TOTAL\", \"8/8\", \"8/8\", \"16/16\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve for NVE threshold classification. AUROC=1.0 in Walker Walk. Cross-env overlay.\"\n    analysis: >\n      1.5x is the Goldilocks threshold: tight enough to catch all CIVO instances (including\n      transient ones like 009b), loose enough to avoid false positives from natural v/e\n      variation. AUROC=1.0 across environments under best tier is the paper's headline\n      quantitative result for C2. The 35K+ time window constraint is essential — report it\n      prominently to prevent misapplication at earlier steps.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Pilot / Extended validation\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\"\n    plan_id: plan_060\n    description: >\n      Pilot for principled CIVO prevention: monitor RSSM prediction uncertainty during\n      imagination rollout, truncate when uncertainty exceeds threshold.\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 580\n      nve_105K: 0.95\n      nve_peak: 1.20\n      collapse: false\n      truncation_rate_mean: 0.35\n      effective_horizon_mean: 5.2\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-37%\"\n      vs_008d_improvement: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Uncertainty-gated truncation addresses the root cause: RSSM predictions degrade at\n      dt>1, so truncating imagination when uncertainty rises prevents the critic from ingesting\n      low-quality predictions. The pilot uses ensemble disagreement or learned variance as\n      the uncertainty signal. At dt=2 with subsample ON, roughly 35% of imagination steps\n      are truncated (those where RSSM uncertainty spikes due to dt>1 predictions), reducing\n      effective horizon from H=8 to ~5.2. NVE stays below 1.5x because the most\n      overestimation-prone imagination steps are removed. However, eval (580) is lower than\n      the dt=1 imagination baseline (870) because truncation reduces learning signal. This\n      is a proof-of-concept — it shows the mechanism is targetable but the current\n      implementation is not competitive with simply using dt=1 imagination.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Learning curve with truncation rate overlay. NVE stays below 1.5x but eval is limited.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Primarily a discussion section contribution: demonstrates that CIVO can be prevented\n      by uncertainty-aware truncation, confirming the causal chain (low-quality RSSM predictions\n      → value overestimation). Not a practical solution yet — the eval gap vs dt=1 imagination\n      suggests room for smarter truncation policies. Frames future work direction.\n\n  - name: \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\"\n    plan_id: plan_040\n    description: >\n      Third DMC locomotion environment. Under best tier, confirms CIVO generalization\n      with different reward structure (sparse contact rewards).\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_mean_peak: 280\n      baseline_ve_105K: 0.58\n      trained_dtEmb_nve_35K_range: [1.5, 3.0]\n      trained_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: \"2/3\"\n      trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate: \"1/3\"\n      no_dtEmb_nve_range: [0.4, 0.8]\n      no_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      cross_env_threshold_accuracy_4env: 0.92\n    result_rationale: >\n      Hopper Hop has a different reward structure from Walker Walk and Cheetah Run — it\n      requires hopping (leaving ground contact repeatedly), with rewards primarily from\n      forward velocity during airborne phases. The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 still applies\n      because multi-step dynamics prediction is challenging regardless of reward structure.\n      The NVE>1.5x rate may be slightly lower (2/3 vs 3/3 in other envs) because Hopper's\n      inherent instability means the baseline v/e ratio may be noisier, creating more\n      variation in NVE denominators. Under best tier, the threshold still achieves ≥3/4\n      separating power per the tier specification. Combined with 3 other environments,\n      cross-env accuracy stays high (0.92+).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"4-environment CIVO summary\"\n        columns: [environment, obs_dim, baseline_peak, trained_above_1.5x, untrained_above_1.5x]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", 24, 950, \"2/2\", \"0/2\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", 17, 850, \"3/3\", \"0/3\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole Swingup\", 5, 847, \"3/3\", \"0/3\"]\n          - [\"Hopper Hop\", 15, 280, \"2/3\", \"0/3\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"4-environment summary figure for paper hero. CIVO confirmed across locomotion + non-locomotion tasks.\"\n    analysis: >\n      P2 priority — strengthens the generalization claim but not strictly needed for CoRL\n      submission (3 environments already sufficient). The lower baseline peak (280) reflects\n      Hopper Hop's difficulty, but the NVE mechanism is independent of absolute return\n      magnitude. If 2/3 instead of 3/3 exceed 1.5x, this creates one false negative — still\n      within the tier-specified ≥3/4 cross-env separating power.\n```\n\n关键观察：\n\n1. **Threshold coverage**：16/16 perfect classification across 3 envs（含 Hopper Hop 则 20/22, accuracy=0.91）\n2. **缺口**：experiment_plan 中没有 Cheetah/Cartpole 的 Full-SGS 实验，但 best tier 要求 \"Full-SGS NVE≤1.0 replicates in Cheetah/PickCube\"。需要新增 plan_025/plan_054。\n3. **Pathway B**：plan_016 预测 1/4 rate，将 008e_v3_s2 从 \"50% failure\" 降级为 \"25% rare event\"，支撑 best tier 叙事。\n4. **plan_030/015 已有数据**：直接使用 actual data 作为 best tier 结果，两者均完美支撑机制链。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2212,"cache_read_input_tokens":75335,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2212},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91b8143b-3ff1-42b2-89c4-d63e7f5bf555","uuid":"1b9dcede-c7dd-4770-a1fb-26370670045e"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":520043,"duration_api_ms":522275,"num_turns":9,"result":"I have all the context needed. Generating the complete best-tier predictions for all 20 planned experiments.\n\n```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  CIVO is a general architectural vulnerability in imagination-based actor-critic with dt-conditioning.\n  Under best tier, the gradient decomposition (93% obs decoder → dt_emb, 7% KL, 0% reward) + Full-SGS\n  (exp_018, NVE=1.00x→0.72x) closes the mechanism at gradient level. Cross-3-environment replication\n  (Walker Walk ✓, Cheetah Run, PickCube/Cartpole) elevates the finding from DreamerV3-specific to\n  structural warning for temporal-abstraction-in-world-models. TAWM contrast (MPC succeeds, imagination\n  fails) is the headline novelty. The 1.5x NVE threshold maintains ≥3/4 separating power in every new\n  environment. Pathway B (008e_v3_s2-type eval-driven collapse) is either rare (≤1/4 cross-env) or gets\n  clean mechanistic characterization via control experiment. Missing from current plan: Full-SGS\n  replication in Cheetah/Cartpole — needed to close \"gradient mechanism generalizes\" claim per tier spec.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Walker Walk — Multi-seed replication\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: >\n      Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds. Registry already has data:\n      009e (seed 1) collapsed NVE peak 4.12x; 009b (seed 2) self-corrected NVE peak 2.05x.\n      Under best tier, both seeds exceed NVE>1.5x (confirmed), collapse is stochastic 1/2 (confirmed).\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 131\n      seed1_nve_peak: 4.12\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55000\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n      seed2_eval_105K: 268\n      seed2_nve_peak: 2.05\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 25000\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction_nve_105K: 0.38\n      both_seeds_exceed_1.5x: true\n      threshold_separating_power: \"2/2 exceed 1.5x at 35K+\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2 (stochastic)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trained dt_emb creates systematic overestimation because the RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2\n      (obs gap 1.30x at inflated absolute levels, reward gap 2.78x) is amplified through the\n      observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient). Every seed with trained\n      dt_emb reliably enters the pathological NVE>1.5x regime because the gradient mechanism is\n      deterministic — the dt_emb learns to differentiate dt values, and the critic exploits this\n      differentiation against inaccurate RSSM predictions. Whether NVE>1.5x becomes sustained (→collapse)\n      or transient (→self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity: replay buffer sampling\n      order, initialization, and whether the actor's policy gradient from real returns can counteract\n      the critic's overestimate before it locks in (primacy bias, Qiao et al. 2023).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"NVE trajectory comparison (seed 1 vs seed 2)\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_nve, seed1_eval, seed2_nve, seed2_eval]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.78, 167, 2.05, 141]\n          - [35000, 1.71, 146, 1.24, 164]\n          - [45000, 2.09, 141, 1.62, 141]\n          - [55000, 4.12, 83, 1.41, 172]\n          - [65000, 1.23, 250, 0.94, 209]\n          - [75000, 1.48, 178, 0.66, 197]\n          - [85000, 1.15, 202, 0.51, 236]\n          - [95000, 1.73, 133, 0.43, 279]\n          - [105000, 1.57, 131, 0.38, 268]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE time series: seed 1 sustained >1.5x (35-55K) → collapse; seed 2 transient >1.5x (25-45K) → self-correction. Shaded 1.5x threshold line.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Actual data confirms best-tier predictions. 2/2 trained dt_emb seeds exceed NVE 1.5x,\n      maintaining perfect threshold separating power (now 4/4 across trained/untrained combined).\n      Collapse stochasticity (1/2) is a feature of the narrative: trained dt_emb is a necessary\n      condition for CIVO, not sufficient for collapse. The sustained-vs-transient NVE persistence\n      distinguishes the two outcomes.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_012\n    description: >\n      Replicate causal control stability. Seed 1 (009f) was killed (infra). Seed 2 (009c)\n      ran to 105K: NVE peaked 1.26x, eval declined to 148. Under best tier, seed 1 re-run\n      shows NVE<1.5x; seed 2 extended to 200K plateaus without crossing 1.5x.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_rerun_eval_105K: 420\n      seed1_rerun_nve_peak: 0.95\n      seed1_rerun_nve_105K: 0.35\n      seed1_rerun_collapse: false\n      seed2_eval_105K: 148\n      seed2_nve_105K: 1.14\n      seed2_eval_200K: 280\n      seed2_nve_200K: 0.85\n      seed2_nve_peak_ever: 1.26\n      seed2_crosses_1.5x: false\n      both_seeds_below_1.5x_at_35K_plus: true\n      threshold_separating_power: \"0/2 exceed 1.5x\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without dt_embedding (dim=0), the critic receives no dt-conditioning information.\n      The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1 from dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 2.78x\n      with trained dt_emb), so the systematic overestimation feedback loop cannot form. Seed 2's\n      rising NVE at 105K (1.14x) reflects value inertia — value_mean was still rising (111.2@105K)\n      while eval declined, but this is fundamentally different from CIVO: the value is simply\n      slow to track a declining eval, not systematically overestimating. Extended to 200K, the\n      value function adjusts downward as it accumulates more accurate return estimates, pulling\n      NVE back below 1.0x. Seed 1 re-run (with stable infrastructure) follows the zigzag\n      recovery pattern of 008f seed 0 (503@105K), because the underlying mechanism is the\n      same — CIVO pathway is structurally blocked.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Seed 2 extended trajectory (predicted 105K-200K)\"\n        columns: [step, eval_return, nve, note]\n        rows:\n          - [105000, 148, 1.14, \"actual\"]\n          - [125000, 195, 1.05, \"value still adjusting\"]\n          - [145000, 240, 0.92, \"value correction begins\"]\n          - [165000, 270, 0.88, \"eval recovering\"]\n          - [185000, 290, 0.85, \"stabilizing\"]\n          - [200000, 280, 0.85, \"plateau\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE trajectory for both seeds with 1.5x threshold line. Demonstrates NVE never crosses 1.5x, contrasting with 008d seeds.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Seed 2's decline after 75K is concerning but does NOT constitute CIVO — NVE never exceeded\n      1.5x, and the value dynamics are qualitatively different (slow value tracking vs value explosion).\n      The 200K extension confirms plateau without threshold crossing. Seed 1 re-run expected to\n      show healthier zigzag recovery (like seed 0), improving cross-seed consistency. Critical for\n      C2: 0/2 untrained seeds cross 1.5x, maintaining 4/4→6/6 threshold separation when combined\n      with plan_011.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_014\n    description: >\n      Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination mitigation. Already done: seed 1 success\n      (869.9@105K), seed 2 collapsed (137@105K) via non-CIVO Pathway B. Under best tier,\n      Pathway B gets clean mechanistic characterization.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 869.9\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.80\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_eval_105K: 137\n      seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n      seed2_collapse: true\n      seed2_pathway: \"B (eval-driven, entropy↑)\"\n      seed2_actor_entropy_collapse: -5.21_to_-3.83\n      pathway_b_rate_walker: \"1/2\"\n      pathway_a_blocked: true\n      gate_dt_mean_both_seeds: 1.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      dt=1 imagination blocks Pathway A (classic CIVO) because the critic never evaluates dt>1\n      imagination steps — the value overestimation feedback loop requires dt>1 predictions where\n      RSSM fidelity degrades. However, the trained dt_embedding still receives gradient from\n      observation decoders during world model training on real dt>1 transitions (subsample ON includes\n      dt=2 in training data). This creates a secondary vulnerability: the dt_emb encodes dt-specific\n      information that subtly corrupts the latent state representation, even when imagination uses only\n      dt=1. Seed 2's collapse shows eval-driven destabilization (715→452→137) with increasing entropy\n      (-5.21→-3.83), the opposite signature of CIVO (which has decreasing entropy). The policy\n      destabilizes because the world model's latent space has been distorted by the trained dt_emb,\n      not because the critic directly overestimates. This Pathway B is stochastic — seed 1 avoids it\n      because its specific initialization leads to a latent representation that is less corrupted by\n      the dt_emb gradients.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\"\n        columns: [feature, pathway_a_008d_s1, pathway_b_008e_v3_s2]\n        rows:\n          - [\"value change at collapse\", \"+130% explosion\", \"+13% mild\"]\n          - [\"eval change at collapse\", \"-43% follows value\", \"-81% leads\"]\n          - [\"NVE driver\", \"value numerator\", \"eval denominator\"]\n          - [\"actor_entropy\", \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88) deterministic\", \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83) random\"]\n          - [\"mechanism\", \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\", \"eval crash → value lag\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-panel: seed 1 healthy trajectory vs seed 2 Pathway B collapse, with entropy overlay showing opposite signatures.\"\n    analysis: >\n      C6 is partially falsified but transformed into a stronger finding: trained dt_embedding\n      creates vulnerability through MULTIPLE pathways. dt=1 imagination eliminates the more\n      understood Pathway A but exposes the previously hidden Pathway B. Under best tier, Pathway B\n      is characterized cleanly enough that it becomes a contribution rather than a weakness — the\n      paper demonstrates that surface-level mitigations are insufficient.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_013\n    description: >\n      Replicate value growth suppression and V-recovery pattern with 2 additional seeds.\n      Seed 0 showed 778@105K (85% baseline), NVE=0.59x with two dip-recovery cycles.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 720\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.90]\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n      seed1_v_recovery_count: 2\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_eval_105K: 690\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.50, 0.95]\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.58\n      seed2_v_recovery_count: 1\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 729\n      cross_seed_mean_nve_105K: 0.60\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-20% (range -15% to -25%)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF, so dt_emb trains but never sees dt>1 data) means the\n      embedding learns from dt=1 transitions only. When imagination encounters dt>1 predictions,\n      the dt_emb provides random-equivalent conditioning for those values. The critic cannot form\n      dt-specific overestimates because the dt_emb carries no learned dt>1 information.\n      NVE stays structurally suppressed (0.5-0.9x) because value growth is bounded by the\n      quality of dt=1-only imagination. The V-recovery pattern (zigzag) arises from periodic\n      exploration-exploitation oscillation in a regime where the critic is conservative.\n      New seeds show similar pattern with ±10% variance in eval (driven by early exploration\n      stochasticity), but the NVE suppression is robust because it's a structural property\n      of the unexposed dt_emb, not a stochastic outcome.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-seed V-recovery summary\"\n        columns: [seed, eval_55K, eval_65K, eval_85K, eval_105K, nve_55K, nve_105K]\n        rows:\n          - [0, 488, 608, 584, 778, 0.93, 0.59]\n          - [1, 450, 570, 620, 720, 0.85, 0.62]\n          - [2, 420, 540, 560, 690, 0.90, 0.58]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"3-seed learning curves with confidence bands, showing V-recovery pattern reproducibility. NVE overlay stays below 1.0x throughout.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms C3 robustly: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection. The ±10% eval\n      variance across seeds is well within DreamerV3's typical seed variability. The key\n      invariant — NVE never exceeds 1.0x at 35K+ — holds across all seeds, consistent with\n      the gradient-level explanation (dt_emb unexposed to dt>1 data → no dt>1-specific\n      gradient signal → no systematic overestimation).\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\"\n    plan_id: plan_015\n    description: >\n      Already run as exp_015b. NVE 0.39-0.47 (far below 1.0), actor_loss→0 (gradient starvation),\n      eval volatile 80-238. Under best tier, this confirms dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition\n      for CIVO, with frozen dt_emb revealing a distinct failure mode (gradient starvation ≠ CIVO).\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 204\n      nve_range: [0.39, 0.47]\n      nve_105K: 0.42\n      actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n      actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n      collapse: false\n      civo_detected: false\n      gradient_starvation: true\n      gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Frozen dt_emb (requires_grad=False, random init) provides constant random conditioning\n      per dt value. The critic cannot learn dt-specific value adjustments → NVE stays far below\n      1.0x (0.39-0.47), confirming CIVO requires TRAINING of dt_emb. However, the frozen random\n      embedding injects constant noise into the actor-critic system: the actor receives\n      confusing value gradients (actor_loss→0 = gradient signal vanishes), and the gate locks\n      to dt=2 100% because the gate network never receives informative gradients to adjust.\n      This produces a distinct failure mode — not value overestimation (CIVO) but gradient\n      starvation from persistent noise injection. The NVE being far below 1.0x (vs >1.5x for\n      trained) is the clean separation: frozen dt_emb → NO CIVO pathway is possible.\n      This completes the 2×2 ablation matrix: trained+present (008d)→CIVO, trained+absent (008f)→no CIVO,\n      frozen+present (015b)→no CIVO but gradient starvation, confirming dt_emb TRAINING is the\n      necessary condition.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"2×2 dt_emb ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H=8)\"\n        columns: [config, dt_emb_status, nve_peak_35K_plus, collapse, failure_mode]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d\", \"trained (grad ON)\", \"4.12x\", \"yes (1/2)\", \"CIVO\"]\n          - [\"008f\", \"absent (dim=0)\", \"1.26x\", \"no\", \"reward degradation\"]\n          - [\"015b\", \"frozen (grad OFF)\", \"0.47x\", \"no\", \"gradient starvation\"]\n          - [\"008c\", \"untrained (subsample OFF)\", \"0.90x\", \"no\", \"learning signal reduction\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"2×2 matrix visualization: NVE heatmap showing only trained+present exceeds 1.5x. Side annotation of distinct failure modes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      015b closes the \"is dt_emb TRAINING necessary?\" question definitively. The 2×2 matrix\n      shows that only trained dt_emb with gradient flow produces NVE>1.5x. All other\n      configurations avoid CIVO but have their own distinct degradation mechanisms — this is\n      the nuanced picture that elevates the paper from \"trained dt_emb is bad\" to \"the gradient\n      pathway through observation decoders→dt_emb is the specific vulnerability.\"\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\"\n    plan_id: plan_016\n    description: >\n      Additional seeds to narrow Pathway B collapse probability. Current rate: 1/2 (seed 1 ok,\n      seed 2 collapsed). Under best tier, Pathway B is rare (≤1/4 cross-env).\n    expected_results:\n      seed3_eval_105K: 840\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.78\n      seed3_collapse: false\n      seed4_eval_105K: 810\n      seed4_nve_105K: 0.82\n      seed4_collapse: false\n      pathway_b_rate_updated: \"1/4 (25%)\"\n      pathway_b_rare_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Under best tier, Pathway B is a rare event. With seeds 3-4 both succeeding, the\n      rate drops from 1/2 to 1/4, consistent with the tier hypothesis that Pathway B is\n      ≤1/4 frequency. The rationale: Pathway B requires a specific conjunction of early\n      training conditions — the trained dt_emb must corrupt the latent representation in a\n      way that destabilizes the actor without triggering the classic CIVO value explosion.\n      This is a narrow failure mode that occurs when the dt_emb gradient from observation\n      decoders creates a latent-space distortion that specifically disrupts the actor's policy\n      gradient, which depends on seed-specific replay buffer composition. Most seeds avoid\n      this because the distortion is mild enough that the actor adapts. The 1/4 rate makes\n      Pathway B a secondary finding — acknowledged and characterized, but not the dominant\n      failure mode (CIVO, Pathway A, remains primary).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"008e_v3 cross-seed summary (4 seeds)\"\n        columns: [seed, eval_105K, nve_105K, collapse, pathway]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 869.9, 0.80, false, \"-\"]\n          - [2, 137, 3.78, true, \"B (eval-driven)\"]\n          - [3, 840, 0.78, false, \"-\"]\n          - [4, 810, 0.82, false, \"-\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"4-seed learning curves with seed 2 collapse highlighted. IQM + 95% CI excluding collapsed seed vs including.\"\n    analysis: >\n      1/4 Pathway B rate is manageable for the narrative: \"dt=1 imagination eliminates the\n      primary CIVO pathway in 4/4 seeds; a secondary eval-driven instability affects 1/4 seeds,\n      characterized by increasing entropy rather than value explosion.\" This positions dt=1\n      imagination as a partial mitigation that eliminates the understood mechanism while leaving\n      a residual vulnerability from the trained dt_emb's latent-space corruption.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Cheetah Run — Cross-environment replication\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_020\n    description: >\n      dt=1 baseline in second DMC environment. Seed 1 (010a) already done: peak 849.8.\n      Under best tier, 3 seeds establish clean reference at ~800-850.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_peak: 849.8\n      seed1_eval_500K: 849.8\n      seed2_eval_peak: 830\n      seed2_eval_500K: 815\n      seed3_eval_peak: 860\n      seed3_eval_500K: 840\n      cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n      cross_seed_std: 15\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.22\n      ve_ratio_65K: 0.50\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.62\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~0.65-0.70 from 135K+\"\n      convergence_step: 120000\n    result_rationale: >\n      DreamerV3 is well-calibrated for Cheetah Run in the original paper. The RSSM handles\n      Cheetah's 17-dim proprioceptive observation well. Baseline v/e calibration should be\n      similar to Walker Walk (~0.65-0.70 at convergence) because the value function learns\n      accurate return estimates when the world model is accurate (dt=1 only). The slightly\n      lower peak than Walker Walk (850 vs 950) reflects Cheetah Run's harder reward landscape\n      (requires coordinated leg movements for running gait). Seed variance ±15 is typical\n      for DreamerV3 on DMC locomotion tasks.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline v/e calibration\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_ve, seed2_ve, seed3_ve, mean_ve]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 0.09, 0.10, 0.08, 0.09]\n          - [35000, 0.22, 0.24, 0.21, 0.22]\n          - [55000, 0.38, 0.40, 0.37, 0.38]\n          - [75000, 0.55, 0.52, 0.56, 0.54]\n          - [105000, 0.62, 0.60, 0.64, 0.62]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed learning curves with IQM and 95% CI bands.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Clean baseline establishes reference for cross-environment CIVO replication. The v/e\n      calibration at ~0.22@35K provides the denominator for NVE threshold calculations in\n      Cheetah Run experiments.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_021\n    description: >\n      Core cross-environment CIVO replication. exp_010b is RUNNING. Under best tier,\n      NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ with collapse in 1-2/3 seeds.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_peak: 380\n      seed1_eval_105K: 95\n      seed1_nve_35K: 1.85\n      seed1_nve_45K: 2.40\n      seed1_nve_55K: 3.10\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed1_collapse_step: 55000\n      seed2_eval_peak: 420\n      seed2_eval_105K: 310\n      seed2_nve_35K: 1.70\n      seed2_nve_45K: 1.55\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.45\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_self_correction: true\n      seed3_eval_peak: 350\n      seed3_eval_105K: 120\n      seed3_nve_35K: 1.90\n      seed3_nve_55K: 2.80\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      cross_seed_nve_above_1.5x_count: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n      cheetah_civo_nve_35K_plus_range: [1.70, 3.10]\n    result_rationale: >\n      The CIVO mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), not task-specific.\n      The same gradient pathway (93% obs decoder→dt_emb) operates in Cheetah Run because the\n      RSSM architecture is identical — only the observation dimensionality differs (17 vs 24 dims).\n      The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 generalizes: Cheetah Run's running dynamics involve rapid\n      velocity changes that are poorly predicted at dt=2 steps, creating the same obs_MSE gap\n      that drives CIVO in Walker Walk. NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ is expected because the v/e\n      calibration baseline is similar (~0.22@35K), and the trained dt_emb introduces the same\n      systematic bias. Collapse rate 2/3 (within best tier range [0.33, 0.67]) because Cheetah's\n      dynamics may be slightly more chaotic, making the self-correction pathway harder to find.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_nve, seed2_nve, seed3_nve, threshold]\n        rows:\n          - [25000, 1.55, 1.40, 1.60, 1.5]\n          - [35000, 1.85, 1.70, 1.90, 1.5]\n          - [45000, 2.40, 1.55, 2.30, 1.5]\n          - [55000, 3.10, 1.20, 2.80, 1.5]\n          - [65000, 3.50, 0.90, 3.20, 1.5]\n          - [75000, null, 0.70, null, 1.5]\n          - [105000, null, 0.45, null, 1.5]\n      - name: \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\"\n        columns: [environment, seeds_above_1.5x, collapse_rate, nve_range_35K_plus]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", \"2/2\", \"1/2\", \"[1.62, 4.12]\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", \"3/3\", \"2/3\", \"[1.70, 3.10]\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-environment NVE comparison panel: Walker Walk (left) vs Cheetah Run (right), with 1.5x threshold. Shows mechanism generalizes.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the critical experiment for the paper. 3/3 seeds exceeding NVE 1.5x in a second\n      environment elevates the finding from \"Walker Walk specific\" to \"RSSM architectural\n      vulnerability.\" The NVE range [1.70, 3.10] falls within the tier-specified [1.6, 3.5].\n      Combined with Walker Walk, the 1.5x threshold now has 5/5 separating power across\n      environments for the \"trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x\" claim.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_022\n    description: >\n      Cross-environment causal control. Under best tier, NVE 0.3-0.9x, no CIVO.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 450\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.40\n      seed2_eval_105K: 380\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.55\n      seed3_eval_105K: 420\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 417\n      cross_seed_nve_range: [0.35, 0.60]\n      seeds_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      collapse_count: 0\n      reward_mae_ratio_vs_baseline: \"~70x\"\n      cheetah_no_dt_emb_nve_35K_plus: [0.35, 0.60]\n    result_rationale: >\n      Removing dt_embedding in Cheetah Run has the same effect as in Walker Walk: the critic\n      cannot exploit dt-conditioned predictions. The reward head loses dt-discriminative power\n      (reward gap collapses to ~1.0x), and NVE stays within normal range because the value\n      function can only track actual return magnitudes, not inflated dt-specific estimates.\n      The absolute eval is lower than baseline (~50% of 850) due to reward prediction degradation\n      (MAE inflated ~70x) — same trade-off as Walker Walk's 008f. NVE range [0.35, 0.60] is\n      within the tier-specified [0.3, 0.9]. The 0/3 result maintains the threshold's cross-env\n      separating power.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cheetah Run no-dt_emb causal control\"\n        columns: [step, seed1_nve, seed2_nve, seed3_nve]\n        rows:\n          - [35000, 0.80, 0.85, 0.78]\n          - [55000, 0.60, 0.70, 0.65]\n          - [75000, 0.45, 0.58, 0.50]\n          - [105000, 0.40, 0.55, 0.45]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run causal control NVE: all 3 seeds stay well below 1.5x threshold.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Combined with plan_021, the cross-environment causal control is complete: 3/3 trained\n      dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/3 no-dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x → 6/6 perfect classification\n      in Cheetah Run. Across Walker Walk + Cheetah Run: 5/5 trained exceed, 0/5 untrained\n      exceed → 10/10 perfect threshold classification. Cross-env threshold accuracy = 1.0,\n      well within tier spec [0.83, 1.0].\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_023\n    description: >\n      V-recovery pattern replication in Cheetah Run. Under best tier, NVE<1.0x with\n      near-baseline recovery.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 710\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.55\n      seed2_eval_105K: 680\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.60\n      seed3_eval_105K: 740\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.52\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 710\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-16%\"\n      nve_range: [0.50, 0.65]\n      v_recovery_pattern: true\n      collapse_count: 0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Same mechanism as Walker Walk 008a: untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → no dt>1 in training\n      data) blocks the CIVO gradient pathway. The dt_emb trains from dt=1 transitions only,\n      providing no dt>1-specific information to the critic. NVE is structurally suppressed\n      below 1.0x. The V-recovery pattern generalizes because it's driven by the exploration-\n      exploitation oscillation in a conservative-critic regime, which is architecture-dependent\n      (RSSM), not task-dependent. Eval at ~84% baseline (710/850) is consistent with Walker\n      Walk's 85% (778/916).\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run 008a-equivalent: 3-seed V-recovery curves with NVE overlay.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Confirms C3 cross-environment: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection in\n      both Walker Walk and Cheetah Run, with consistent NVE suppression (0.5-0.6x) and\n      eval recovery to ~85% baseline. The mechanism (unexposed dt_emb → no dt>1 gradient\n      → no systematic overestimation) is environment-agnostic.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Cartpole Swingup — Third environment\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_050\n    description: >\n      dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment. Broadens scope beyond locomotion\n      to test whether CIVO depends on movement dynamics.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_peak: 845\n      seed2_eval_peak: 860\n      seed3_eval_peak: 835\n      cross_seed_mean_peak: 847\n      cross_seed_std: 13\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.23\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.60\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~0.63-0.68\"\n      convergence_step: 100000\n    result_rationale: >\n      DreamerV3 handles Cartpole Swingup well — 5-dim observation and 1-dim action make it a\n      simpler learning problem than locomotion. The RSSM achieves good prediction quality\n      rapidly. Baseline peaks at ~840-860 with tight cross-seed variance (~±13) because the\n      task has lower stochasticity than locomotion. The v/e calibration (~0.23@35K, ~0.63@convergence)\n      provides the reference for NVE calculations.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole Swingup baseline learning curves with v/e calibration.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Establishes clean reference. Cartpole's simpler observation space (5-dim) tests whether\n      CIVO still occurs when the RSSM has less room for fidelity degradation — the key question\n      is whether even small absolute errors in dt>1 prediction, amplified by trained dt_emb, are\n      sufficient to trigger NVE>1.5x.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_051\n    description: >\n      CIVO trigger in non-locomotion environment. Under best tier, NVE>1.5x in ≥2/3 seeds.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_35K: 1.65\n      seed1_nve_55K: 2.20\n      seed1_eval_105K: 150\n      seed1_collapse: true\n      seed2_nve_35K: 1.55\n      seed2_nve_55K: 1.40\n      seed2_eval_105K: 380\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed3_nve_35K: 1.75\n      seed3_nve_55K: 2.50\n      seed3_eval_105K: 120\n      seed3_collapse: true\n      seeds_above_1.5x_35K_plus: \"3/3\"\n      collapse_rate: \"2/3\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Even with Cartpole's simpler 5-dim observation, the RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is\n      sufficient to trigger CIVO. The mechanism operates through the observation decoder→dt_emb\n      gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient), not through observation dimensionality per se.\n      A 5-dim observation still has enough structure for the dt_emb to learn dt-specific\n      representations that the RSSM can't support. The pole angle and angular velocity components\n      are particularly sensitive to temporal skipping — Cartpole dynamics are chaotic near the\n      inverted position, so dt=2 predictions of these components degrade substantially.\n      NVE>1.5x in 3/3 seeds maintains the perfect threshold separating power. Collapse in 2/3\n      is within the tier-specified range.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Cross-environment CIVO summary (3 envs)\"\n        columns: [environment, obs_dim, seeds_above_1.5x, collapse_rate]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", 24, \"2/2\", \"1/2\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", 17, \"3/3\", \"2/3\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole Swingup\", 5, \"3/3\", \"2/3\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"3-environment CIVO comparison: NVE trajectories across Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CIVO triggers in 3/3 environments regardless of observation dimensionality. This is\n      the strongest evidence that CIVO is an architectural vulnerability of RSSM + trained\n      dt_emb, not a task-specific phenomenon. environments_with_confirmed_civo = 3, matching\n      the tier specification.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_052\n    description: >\n      Causal control replication in third environment.\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.50\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.60\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.45\n      seeds_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      cross_seed_mean_eval_105K: 480\n      collapse_count: 0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Same causal mechanism as Walker Walk and Cheetah Run: no dt_embedding → no dt-conditioned\n      value exploitation → NVE stays within normal range. The result is expected to be robust\n      because the causal pathway (trained dt_emb → observation decoder gradient → systematic\n      overestimation) is completely removed by setting dt_embed_dim=0. Cross-environment\n      consistency of this control strengthens the causal claim from \"observed in one env\"\n      to \"architecturally guaranteed.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole Swingup causal control: NVE well below 1.5x for all seeds.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Across 3 environments: trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x in 8/8 seeds; no dt_emb → NVE<1.5x\n      in 0/8 seeds. Threshold classification accuracy = 16/16 = 1.0. This is the paper's\n      strongest quantitative result.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Analysis experiments (0-1 GPU hour)\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\"\n    plan_id: plan_030\n    description: >\n      Already completed (exp_rssm_fidelity_analysis). Under best tier, results provide\n      the mechanistic foundation showing RSSM fidelity gap drives CIVO.\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_obs_gap: 2.04\n      baseline_reward_gap: 1.56\n      008d_obs_gap: 1.30\n      008d_obs_dt1_absolute: 41.31\n      008d_obs_dt2_absolute: 53.67\n      008d_reward_gap: 2.78\n      008f_obs_gap: 2.75\n      008f_reward_gap: 1.01\n      008d_dt1_obs_vs_baseline: \"3.06x (41.31 / 13.49)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The RSSM fidelity analysis reveals the root cause: dt=2 predictions are systematically\n      worse than dt=1 across all models. The trained dt_emb model (008d) shows paradoxically\n      low obs_gap ratio (1.30x) because its dt=1 absolute error is already 3x baseline (model\n      has degraded overall). The reward gap (2.78x) in 008d is the smoking gun — the trained\n      dt_emb enables the reward head to make dt-specific predictions, but these predictions are\n      systematically biased at dt=2 due to the underlying RSSM fidelity gap. In 008f (no dt_emb),\n      reward gap collapses to 1.01x — the reward head treats dt=1 and dt=2 identically, preventing\n      systematic bias. This explains why 008f avoids CIVO despite higher absolute reward MAE.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"RSSM Fidelity (absolute + ratio)\"\n        columns: [model, dt1_obs_MSE, dt2_obs_MSE, obs_gap, dt1_reward_MAE, dt2_reward_MAE, reward_gap]\n        rows:\n          - [\"baseline\", 13.49, 27.50, \"2.04x\", 0.0102, 0.0159, \"1.56x\"]\n          - [\"008d (trained)\", 41.31, 53.67, \"1.30x\", 0.0568, 0.1579, \"2.78x\"]\n          - [\"008f (no dt_emb)\", 8.01, 22.04, \"2.75x\", 0.7469, 0.7526, \"1.01x\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: absolute obs_MSE and reward_MAE at dt=1 vs dt=2 for three models, with gap ratio annotated.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Must present absolute values AND ratios side-by-side. 008d's 1.30x obs gap is misleading\n      without the absolute context (41.31 at dt=1 = 3x baseline). The reward gap (2.78x)\n      directly explains CIVO: trained dt_emb creates dt-specific reward biases that the\n      critic amplifies through the imagination rollout.\n\n  - name: \"Actor entropy analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_031\n    description: >\n      0 GPU cost — extract from existing metrics.jsonl logs. Separates CIVO (Pathway A)\n      from non-CIVO (Pathway B) collapse signatures.\n    expected_results:\n      008d_s1_entropy_35K: -3.47\n      008d_s1_entropy_55K: -4.88\n      008d_s1_entropy_direction: \"decreasing (policy → deterministic)\"\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n      008e_v3_s2_entropy_direction: \"increasing (policy → random)\"\n      008a_entropy_stable_range: \"[-4.5, -3.8]\"\n      008c_entropy_stable_range: \"[-3.5, -2.8]\"\n      baseline_entropy_converged: -5.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Actor entropy is the cleanest discriminator between Pathway A and Pathway B. In classic\n      CIVO (Pathway A, 008d), the overestimating critic provides strong gradients toward a\n      narrow set of actions → entropy decreases as the policy becomes overconfident in\n      the wrong actions. In Pathway B (008e_v3_s2), the policy destabilizes from latent-space\n      corruption → entropy increases as the actor loses confidence and explores randomly.\n      Healthy configs (008a, 008c) maintain stable entropy in a moderate range, reflecting\n      properly calibrated exploration-exploitation. The baseline converges to low entropy\n      (~-5.2) reflecting a well-learned deterministic policy, which is the correct behavior\n      for Walker Walk.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Actor entropy at collapse/stability\"\n        columns: [config, entropy_at_collapse_onset, entropy_direction, pathway]\n        rows:\n          - [\"008d s1 (CIVO)\", -3.47, \"↓ to -4.88\", \"A\"]\n          - [\"008e_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)\", -5.21, \"↑ to -3.83\", \"B\"]\n          - [\"008a (healthy)\", -4.2, \"stable ±0.3\", \"-\"]\n          - [\"baseline\", -5.0, \"stable ±0.2\", \"-\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Actor entropy time series for collapsed vs healthy configs. Divergent arrows at collapse onset distinguish Pathway A (↓) from B (↑).\"\n    analysis: >\n      This figure is essential for the dual-pathway narrative. It provides the most intuitive\n      evidence that 008d and 008e_v3_s2 fail through different mechanisms, justifying the\n      Pathway A / Pathway B framing.\n\n  - name: \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\"\n    plan_id: plan_032\n    description: >\n      1 GPU hour — load checkpoints and evaluate. Shows distribution-level evidence\n      for CIVO beyond the scalar v/e ratio.\n    expected_results:\n      008d_35K_value_mean: 60.2\n      008d_35K_return_mean: 146.3\n      008d_35K_value_skewness: 1.8\n      008d_55K_value_mean: 137.9\n      008d_55K_return_mean: 82.8\n      008d_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.85\n      008a_55K_value_mean: 183.5\n      008a_55K_return_mean: 487.9\n      008a_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.35\n      baseline_55K_value_above_return_frac: 0.40\n    result_rationale: >\n      The scalar v/e ratio is a proxy; distribution plots show where the divergence occurs.\n      In 008d at 55K (pre-collapse), the value distribution is right-skewed with 85% of\n      value predictions exceeding realized returns — a systematic overestimation signature.\n      In 008a (healthy, untrained dt_emb), the value distribution is symmetric around returns\n      with only 35% exceeding (slight underestimation, consistent with conservative critic).\n      The distribution shape directly shows the CIVO mechanism: trained dt_emb creates a\n      population of states where the critic is systematically optimistic, particularly for\n      states that would be reached via dt>1 imagination.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"2×2 panel: V(s) vs MC-return scatter for 008d/008a at 35K/55K. Diagonal = perfect calibration. 008d@55K shows systematic departure above diagonal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Addresses R3 (v/e is crude proxy) directly. The distribution plots provide a more\n      nuanced view: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate all values — it specifically affects\n      states reachable via dt>1 imagination, creating a bimodal distribution.\n\n  - name: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_033\n    description: >\n      0 GPU cost — extract dt_emb weights from checkpoints. Shows how dt=1 and dt=2\n      embeddings diverge during training.\n    expected_results:\n      008d_cosine_sim_5K: 0.92\n      008d_cosine_sim_25K: 0.65\n      008d_cosine_sim_35K: 0.42\n      008d_cosine_sim_55K: 0.28\n      008a_cosine_sim_55K: 0.88\n      008f_cosine_sim: \"N/A (no dt_emb)\"\n      015b_cosine_sim: 0.92\n      baseline_cosine_sim: \"N/A (no dt_emb)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trained dt_emb (008d) starts with similar dt=1/dt=2 embeddings (cosine ~0.92 from\n      random init) and diverges rapidly as the observation decoder gradient differentiates\n      them. By 35K (when NVE first exceeds 1.5x), cosine similarity has dropped to ~0.42 —\n      the embeddings are now substantially different, enabling the critic to make highly\n      dt-specific (and wrong) predictions. In 008a (subsample OFF, untrained on dt>1 data),\n      cosine similarity stays high (~0.88) because the gradient signal only comes from dt=1\n      transitions, providing no pressure to differentiate. In 015b (frozen), cosine similarity\n      stays at initialization (~0.92). The divergence timeline correlates with NVE onset,\n      providing indirect evidence that embedding differentiation drives CIVO.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"dt_emb cosine similarity evolution\"\n        columns: [step, \"008d (trained)\", \"008a (unexposed)\", \"015b (frozen)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [5000, 0.92, 0.92, 0.92]\n          - [15000, 0.80, 0.90, 0.92]\n          - [25000, 0.65, 0.89, 0.92]\n          - [35000, 0.42, 0.88, 0.92]\n          - [55000, 0.28, 0.88, 0.92]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cosine similarity decay plot with NVE threshold crossing annotated on the 008d curve.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Provides a mechanistic visualization of WHY trained dt_emb creates the vulnerability:\n      gradient-driven embedding divergence → dt-specific critic predictions → CIVO.\n      The correlation between divergence and NVE onset is compelling but not causal proof\n      by itself — it complements the gradient decomposition (D061) and Full-SGS (018) evidence.\n\n  - name: \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_034\n    description: >\n      0 GPU cost — computed from existing metrics. Tests robustness of 1.5x threshold\n      across all available data.\n    expected_results:\n      threshold_1.3x_accuracy: 0.75\n      threshold_1.3x_false_positives: 2\n      threshold_1.5x_accuracy: 1.0\n      threshold_1.5x_false_positives: 0\n      threshold_1.5x_false_negatives: 0\n      threshold_1.7x_accuracy: 0.875\n      threshold_1.7x_false_negatives: 1\n      threshold_2.0x_accuracy: 0.75\n      threshold_2.0x_false_negatives: 2\n      auroc_walker_walk: 1.0\n      optimal_threshold: 1.5\n      cross_env_threshold_accuracy: 1.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 1.5x, the current Walker Walk data has 4/4 perfect classification (2/2 trained above,\n      2/2 untrained below). At 1.3x, two untrained seeds (009c@105K=1.14x, 008a somewhere near\n      1.0x) could create false positives if the window is shifted earlier. At 1.7x, 009b's\n      peak of 1.62x@45K would be missed (false negative). At 2.0x, both 009b (2.05x@25K but\n      this is pre-35K) and potentially 008d at 35K (1.71x) would be missed. The 1.5x threshold\n      at 35K+ is the optimal operating point because: (a) baseline v/e has converged sufficiently\n      by 35K (>0.24, making ratios reliable), (b) 1.5x is approximately 2σ above baseline v/e\n      natural variation, and (c) it achieves perfect sensitivity and specificity in the current\n      data. Cross-environment accuracy stays at 1.0 under best tier because the mechanism is\n      architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), so the threshold generalizes.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Threshold sensitivity (Walker Walk, 35K+ window)\"\n        columns: [threshold, TP, FP, TN, FN, accuracy, notes]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1.3x\", 2, 1, 1, 0, \"3/4=0.75\", \"009c@105K=1.14x → FP edge case\"]\n          - [\"1.5x\", 2, 0, 2, 0, \"4/4=1.00\", \"optimal\"]\n          - [\"1.7x\", 1, 0, 2, 1, \"3/4=0.75\", \"009b peak 1.62x missed\"]\n          - [\"2.0x\", 1, 0, 2, 1, \"3/4=0.75\", \"only sustained CIVO detected\"]\n      - name: \"Cross-environment threshold accuracy (best tier, 1.5x)\"\n        columns: [environment, trained_above, untrained_below, accuracy]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", \"2/2\", \"2/2\", \"4/4\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", \"3/3\", \"3/3\", \"6/6\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole Swingup\", \"3/3\", \"3/3\", \"6/6\"]\n          - [\"TOTAL\", \"8/8\", \"8/8\", \"16/16\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve for NVE threshold classification. AUROC=1.0 in Walker Walk. Cross-env overlay.\"\n    analysis: >\n      1.5x is the Goldilocks threshold: tight enough to catch all CIVO instances (including\n      transient ones like 009b), loose enough to avoid false positives from natural v/e\n      variation. AUROC=1.0 across environments under best tier is the paper's headline\n      quantitative result for C2. The 35K+ time window constraint is essential — report it\n      prominently to prevent misapplication at earlier steps.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # Pilot / Extended validation\n  # ============================================================\n\n  - name: \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\"\n    plan_id: plan_060\n    description: >\n      Pilot for principled CIVO prevention: monitor RSSM prediction uncertainty during\n      imagination rollout, truncate when uncertainty exceeds threshold.\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 580\n      nve_105K: 0.95\n      nve_peak: 1.20\n      collapse: false\n      truncation_rate_mean: 0.35\n      effective_horizon_mean: 5.2\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-37%\"\n      vs_008d_improvement: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      Uncertainty-gated truncation addresses the root cause: RSSM predictions degrade at\n      dt>1, so truncating imagination when uncertainty rises prevents the critic from ingesting\n      low-quality predictions. The pilot uses ensemble disagreement or learned variance as\n      the uncertainty signal. At dt=2 with subsample ON, roughly 35% of imagination steps\n      are truncated (those where RSSM uncertainty spikes due to dt>1 predictions), reducing\n      effective horizon from H=8 to ~5.2. NVE stays below 1.5x because the most\n      overestimation-prone imagination steps are removed. However, eval (580) is lower than\n      the dt=1 imagination baseline (870) because truncation reduces learning signal. This\n      is a proof-of-concept — it shows the mechanism is targetable but the current\n      implementation is not competitive with simply using dt=1 imagination.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Learning curve with truncation rate overlay. NVE stays below 1.5x but eval is limited.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Primarily a discussion section contribution: demonstrates that CIVO can be prevented\n      by uncertainty-aware truncation, confirming the causal chain (low-quality RSSM predictions\n      → value overestimation). Not a practical solution yet — the eval gap vs dt=1 imagination\n      suggests room for smarter truncation policies. Frames future work direction.\n\n  - name: \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\"\n    plan_id: plan_040\n    description: >\n      Third DMC locomotion environment. Under best tier, confirms CIVO generalization\n      with different reward structure (sparse contact rewards).\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_mean_peak: 280\n      baseline_ve_105K: 0.58\n      trained_dtEmb_nve_35K_range: [1.5, 3.0]\n      trained_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: \"2/3\"\n      trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate: \"1/3\"\n      no_dtEmb_nve_range: [0.4, 0.8]\n      no_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x: \"0/3\"\n      cross_env_threshold_accuracy_4env: 0.92\n    result_rationale: >\n      Hopper Hop has a different reward structure from Walker Walk and Cheetah Run — it\n      requires hopping (leaving ground contact repeatedly), with rewards primarily from\n      forward velocity during airborne phases. The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 still applies\n      because multi-step dynamics prediction is challenging regardless of reward structure.\n      The NVE>1.5x rate may be slightly lower (2/3 vs 3/3 in other envs) because Hopper's\n      inherent instability means the baseline v/e ratio may be noisier, creating more\n      variation in NVE denominators. Under best tier, the threshold still achieves ≥3/4\n      separating power per the tier specification. Combined with 3 other environments,\n      cross-env accuracy stays high (0.92+).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"4-environment CIVO summary\"\n        columns: [environment, obs_dim, baseline_peak, trained_above_1.5x, untrained_above_1.5x]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Walker Walk\", 24, 950, \"2/2\", \"0/2\"]\n          - [\"Cheetah Run\", 17, 850, \"3/3\", \"0/3\"]\n          - [\"Cartpole Swingup\", 5, 847, \"3/3\", \"0/3\"]\n          - [\"Hopper Hop\", 15, 280, \"2/3\", \"0/3\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"4-environment summary figure for paper hero. CIVO confirmed across locomotion + non-locomotion tasks.\"\n    analysis: >\n      P2 priority — strengthens the generalization claim but not strictly needed for CoRL\n      submission (3 environments already sufficient). The lower baseline peak (280) reflects\n      Hopper Hop's difficulty, but the NVE mechanism is independent of absolute return\n      magnitude. If 2/3 instead of 3/3 exceed 1.5x, this creates one false negative — still\n      within the tier-specified ≥3/4 cross-env separating power.\n```\n\n关键观察：\n\n1. **Threshold coverage**：16/16 perfect classification across 3 envs（含 Hopper Hop 则 20/22, accuracy=0.91）\n2. **缺口**：experiment_plan 中没有 Cheetah/Cartpole 的 Full-SGS 实验，但 best tier 要求 \"Full-SGS NVE≤1.0 replicates in Cheetah/PickCube\"。需要新增 plan_025/plan_054。\n3. **Pathway B**：plan_016 预测 1/4 rate，将 008e_v3_s2 从 \"50% failure\" 降级为 \"25% rare event\"，支撑 best tier 叙事。\n4. **plan_030/015 已有数据**：直接使用 actual data 作为 best tier 结果，两者均完美支撑机制链。","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"91b8143b-3ff1-42b2-89c4-d63e7f5bf555","total_cost_usd":1.2996715,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_inp